// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_TIMAEUS: Record<string, string> = {
  "greek.tim.17a": "Socrates counts his guests and asks Timaeus where the fourth one from yesterday is, since today those guests are meant to be the hosts. Timaeus says the man has fallen ill; he would never have missed the meeting by choice. Socrates says the rest of them will have to make up for him, and Timaeus agrees they will do their best.",
  "greek.tim.17b": "Timaeus says it would be wrong not to return the fine welcome Socrates gave them yesterday. Socrates asks whether they remember the topics he set them. Timaeus says they remember some of it, and Socrates can remind them of the rest; better still, he asks Socrates to run through it again briefly so it sticks.",
  "greek.tim.17c": "Socrates agrees. He says yesterday's talk was mainly about the best kind of constitution for a city and what its citizens should be like, and Timaeus says they all liked it. Socrates recalls that they began by separating the farmers and craftsmen from the class that defends the city, and that each citizen was given",
  "greek.tim.17d": "a single job suited to their nature. The fighters, Socrates goes on, were to be nothing but guardians of the city, protecting it from attackers outside and troublemakers inside. They were to be gentle in judging the people under them, who are their natural friends,",
  "greek.tim.18a": "but tough in battle against any enemy. Timaeus agrees. Socrates recalls saying that the guardians must have souls that are both very spirited and very fond of wisdom, so they can be gentle with friends and hard on enemies. He also recalls that they were to be trained in physical exercise, music, and every suitable subject, and Timaeus agrees.",
  "greek.tim.18b": "Socrates says these trained guardians were never to own silver, gold or anything else privately. In return for guarding the city, they would receive a modest wage from the people they protect, enough for sensible people, and would spend it together, living in common and devoting themselves to being good, with no other occupations. Timaeus agrees that this was said.",
  "greek.tim.18c": "Socrates recalls that women were to be trained in harmony with the men and given exactly the same work, in war and in everything else. Timaeus confirms it. Socrates then asks about having children, which should be easy to remember because the proposal was so unusual: marriages and children were to be shared by everyone, so that no one would know which children were their own, and everyone would treat everyone",
  "greek.tim.18d": "as real family: people of the same age as brothers and sisters, older people as parents and grandparents, younger ones as children and grandchildren. Timaeus agrees this is easy to remember. Socrates goes on: so that the citizens would turn out as good as possible by nature, the rulers, men and women, were to arrange marriages through a secret kind of lottery,",
  "greek.tim.18e": "so that good men would be paired with good women and bad with bad, and nobody would feel resentful, because they would put the pairings down to luck. Timaeus says they remember this.",
  "greek.tim.19a": "Socrates recalls that the children of good parents were to be raised in the city, while those of bad parents were to be quietly sent to other parts of the State. As they grew up, the rulers would watch for any who deserved to come back and bring them back, sending away in their place any undeserving children from their own group. Timaeus agrees. Socrates asks whether this is a fair summary of yesterday's talk or whether anything is missing.",
  "greek.tim.19b": "Timaeus says nothing is missing; that is exactly what was said. Socrates then explains how he feels about the city they described. It is like seeing beautiful animals, whether in a painting or alive but standing still, and longing to see them move and show what they can do in some suitable exercise.",
  "greek.tim.19c": "That, says Socrates, is how he feels about their city. He would love to hear someone describe it in words competing with other cities in the struggles cities face: going to war in the right spirit, and showing, in both fighting and negotiating, the qualities its education gave it. He then turns to Critias and Hermocrates,",
  "greek.tim.19d": "admitting that he himself could never praise their citizens and city well enough. That is not surprising, he says, but he thinks the same of the poets, past and present. He means them no disrespect, but imitators find it easiest to copy what they grew up among; it is hard to imitate well in action anything outside one's own upbringing,",
  "greek.tim.19e": "and harder still in words. As for the Sophists, Socrates says he respects their skill at speaking on many topics, but because they wander from city to city with no home of their own, he fears they would get it wrong about men who are both philosophers and statesmen, and what such men would say and do in war. So, he says, that leaves people like his listeners,",
  "greek.tim.20a": "who share both qualities by nature and by training. Timaeus comes from Locris in Italy, a very well-run city, where he is second to none in wealth or rank; he has held its highest offices and, Socrates thinks, has reached the top in every branch of philosophy. Everyone knows Critias is no beginner in these subjects. And many people say that Hermocrates, by nature and upbringing,",
  "greek.tim.20b": "is up to all these questions. With that in mind, Socrates says, he gladly explained his ideal city yesterday when they asked, because he knew no one living could better take up the next topic: showing the city fighting a fitting war and displaying all its qualities. So, having given his own talk, he set them this topic in turn. And they, after talking it over together,",
  "greek.tim.20c": "agreed to repay him today with a feast of speeches, so here he is, dressed for the occasion and keen to begin. Hermocrates says they will not hold back and have no excuse to refuse. In fact, as soon as they got back to Critias's house where they are staying, and even on the way there, they were already discussing these subjects.",
  "greek.tim.20d": "Hermocrates says Critias told them an old traditional story, and asks him to tell it again for Socrates so they can decide whether it fits their topic. Critias says he will, if Timaeus agrees, and Timaeus does. Critias tells Socrates to listen to a tale that is very strange but entirely true, as Solon,",
  "greek.tim.20e": "the wisest of the Seven Wise Men, once said. Critias explains that Solon, as he often mentions in his poems, was a relative and close friend of Critias's great-grandfather Dropides. Dropides told Critias's grandfather, also called Critias, who told it to the speaker in turn: that long ago this city did great and wonderful things, now forgotten because of time and the deaths of the people involved. The greatest of these would be fitting",
  "greek.tim.21a": "to tell now, both to repay Socrates and as a hymn of praise to the Goddess on her festival day. Socrates is delighted and asks what this deed was, which Solon reported and the elder Critias passed on, a real event that was never written down. Critias says he will tell it: it is an old story, which he heard from an old man. At that time, he says,",
  "greek.tim.21b": "his grandfather Critias was nearly ninety and he himself was about ten. It was the day of the Apaturia festival called Cureotis, when the boys' ceremony was held, and the fathers set up recitation contests. Many poets' works were recited, and because Solon's poems were new then, many of the children sang them. One man from their clan, either sincerely or to flatter",
  "greek.tim.21c": "old Critias, said he thought Solon was not only the wisest of men but also the finest of all poets. The old man was very pleased, Critias remembers, and smiled and said: if only, Amynander, Solon had treated poetry as serious work instead of a hobby, and had finished the story he brought back from Egypt, instead of having to drop it because of the civil strife and troubles he found at home,",
  "greek.tim.21d": "then no poet, not Hesiod or Homer or anyone else, would have been more famous. Amynander asked what the story was. Critias said it was about the greatest of all deeds, done by this city, though the record of it had been lost through time and the deaths of those who did it. Amynander asked him to tell from the start what Solon said, how he heard it, and who his sources were.",
  "greek.tim.21e": "Critias said that in the Egyptian Delta, where the Nile splits in two, there is a district called the Saitic, whose main city is Sais, home of King Amasis. The people there say their city was founded by a goddess called Neith in Egyptian and, they claim, Athena in Greek. They say they love Athens and are in some way related to its people. Solon said he was highly respected there, and when he questioned the priests",
  "greek.tim.22a": "who knew most about ancient history, he found that neither he nor any other Greek knew practically anything about the distant past. Once, to get them talking about ancient times, he told them the oldest Greek stories: about Phoroneus, said to be the first man, and Niobe; then the legend of Deucalion and Pyrrha surviving the Flood, and the family tree of their descendants;",
  "greek.tim.22b": "Solon also tried to work out dates by counting up the years these events covered. Then one of the priests, a very old man, said: 'Solon, Solon, you Greeks are always children; there is no such thing as an old Greek.' Solon asked what he meant. The priest said that all Greeks are young in mind, because they have no beliefs handed down from ancient tradition and no knowledge that is truly old.",
  "greek.tim.22c": "The priest explains why: humanity has been wiped out many times in different ways, and will be again, most of all by fire and by water, and in lesser ways by countless other means. Both Greeks and Egyptians tell the story of Phaethon, son of Helios, who took his father's chariot, could not steer it on his father's path, burned up everything on earth, and was killed by a thunderbolt. That sounds like a legend, the priest says, but the truth behind it",
  "greek.tim.22d": "is that the bodies moving in the sky around the earth sometimes drift off course, and at long intervals the things on earth are destroyed by great fire. Then people living on mountains and in high, dry places suffer more than those near rivers or the sea; in Egypt the Nile, which saves them in other ways too, rises and saves them from this. But when the gods cleanse the earth with a flood, the herdsmen and shepherds in the mountains survive,",
  "greek.tim.22e": "while people in Greek cities are swept into the sea by the rivers. In Egypt, the priest says, water never pours down onto the fields from above, then or at any time; it naturally rises up from below. That is why the records kept in Egypt are counted the oldest. In fact, wherever it is not too hot or too cold, there are always some people living, sometimes more, sometimes fewer.",
  "greek.tim.23a": "The priest goes on: any great, noble or remarkable event, whether in Greece, in Egypt or anywhere else they have heard of, has been written down long ago and kept in the Egyptian temples. But the Greeks and others have only just got writing and the other skills a civilized city needs, when, after the usual number of years, the flood from heaven sweeps down on them again like a plague",
  "greek.tim.23b": "and leaves only people who cannot read or write, so they start again as young as ever, knowing nothing of what happened long ago, there or in Egypt. The family histories Solon just told, the priest says, are hardly better than children's stories. The Greeks remember only one flood, though there were many before it. And they do not know that the finest and most excellent race of people was born in their own land, and that Solon and his whole",
  "greek.tim.23c": "city are descended from a small remnant of them that happened to survive. The Greeks have forgotten this because for many generations the survivors died without being able to write anything down. In fact, Solon, the priest says, before the greatest flood, the city that is now Athens was the bravest in war and the best governed in every other way. It is said to have had the most splendid works and the finest constitution of any people known.",
  "greek.tim.23d": "Solon was amazed and eagerly begged the priest to tell him everything about those ancient citizens, in order and in detail. The priest said he would gladly do so, for Solon's sake and his city's, and most of all for the sake of the Goddess who adopted both Athens and Sais as her own, and raised and taught them. She founded Athens first, a thousand years earlier, taking its seed from Ge",
  "greek.tim.23e": "and Hephaestus, and Sais after that. The priest says their sacred writings record Egyptian civilization as 8,000 years old. So he will briefly tell Solon some of the laws and the finest deeds of the Athenians who lived 9,000 years ago.",
  "greek.tim.24a": "The full account, in exact order and detail, they can go through later using the actual writings. To see what those laws were like, the priest says, look at Egypt's laws today, since many of the old Athenian laws can still be found here. First the priests are kept separate from everyone else; then the craftsmen, each trade working on its own without mixing; then the shepherds, hunters and farmers, each a separate class. The warrior class here, too,",
  "greek.tim.24b": "as Solon has surely noticed, is kept apart from the others, and the law tells it to do nothing but train for war. Their weapons are shields and spears, which the Egyptians were the first in Asia to use, taught by the Goddess, just as she taught the Athenians first in their part of the world. And as for learning, the priest says, Solon can see how much attention the law here",
  "greek.tim.24c": "has paid from the start to the order of the universe, working out all the effects that divine causes have on human life, down to prophecy and medicine for health, and mastering all the related studies. So, the priest says, back then the Goddess gave the Athenians this whole orderly system before anyone else, and founded their city, choosing the place where they were born because she saw that its climate was well balanced and would produce very wise people.",
  "greek.tim.24d": "The Goddess, who loves both war and wisdom, chose the place most likely to produce people like herself and founded it first. So the Athenians lived under laws like these, and even better ones, and outdid everyone in every kind of excellence, as you would expect of children and pupils of the gods. Many great deeds of their city are recorded in Egypt and amaze people, the priest says, but one stands out above them all",
  "greek.tim.24e": "for its size and nobility. The records say that Athens once stopped a huge army that set out from far off in the Atlantic Ocean and was arrogantly marching against all of Europe and Asia too. In those days the ocean could be crossed. In front of the strait the Greeks call 'the pillars of Heracles' there was an island bigger than Libya and Asia put together, and travellers could go from it to other islands, and from those to the whole continent",
  "greek.tim.25a": "on the far side that surrounds that true ocean. The sea inside the strait, the priest says, is really just a harbour with a narrow entrance; the sea beyond is a real ocean, and the land around it truly deserves to be called a continent. On this island of Atlantis there was a powerful and remarkable alliance of kings, who ruled the whole island, many other islands, and parts of the continent. Besides that,",
  "greek.tim.25b": "inside the strait they ruled Libya as far as Egypt and Europe as far as Tuscany. This whole force gathered together and tried, in one attack, to enslave Athens, Egypt and every land inside the strait. Then, Solon, the priest says, the men of Athens showed the whole world their courage and strength. Their city was the best of all",
  "greek.tim.25c": "in bravery and the skills of war. Sometimes leading the Greeks, sometimes left alone when all the others deserted it, Athens faced the gravest dangers, beat the invaders and set up a victory monument. It saved from slavery those not yet enslaved, and generously freed everyone else living inside the pillars of Heracles. But later there were terrible earthquakes and floods,",
  "greek.tim.25d": "and in one dreadful day and night all the Athenian warriors were swallowed by the earth, and the island of Atlantis likewise sank into the sea and disappeared. That is why the sea there still cannot be crossed or explored: it is blocked by shallow mud left by the sinking island. Critias tells Socrates that this is a short outline of what the elder Critias heard from Solon.",
  "greek.tim.25e": "Critias says that when Socrates was describing his city and citizens yesterday, he was amazed to remember this story, and struck by the strange luck that Socrates' description matched Solon's account so closely in most points. But he did not want",
  "greek.tim.26a": "to tell it straight away, because after so long he did not remember it clearly enough, and he thought he should first go over it all carefully in his mind. That is why he happily agreed to the topic Socrates set, thinking they would have good material for a satisfying speech, which is always the hardest part. So, as Hermocrates said, as soon as they left Socrates yesterday he began telling the others the story as he remembered it,",
  "greek.tim.26b": "and after they parted he thought it over during the night and got almost all of it back. It is amazing, Critias says, how firmly what we learn as children sticks in the mind. He is not sure he could recall everything he heard yesterday, but he would be very surprised if he has forgotten a single detail of the story he heard so long ago. He loved hearing it at the time,",
  "greek.tim.26c": "and the old man was happy to tell it because Critias kept asking him questions, so it is fixed in his memory like a permanent painting. He also told it to the others first thing this morning so they could share his supply of material. So now, he says, he is ready to tell the story in full, just as he heard it, not just in outline. And the city and citizens Socrates described yesterday as if in a made-up tale",
  "greek.tim.26d": "they will now bring into the real world. They will suppose that Socrates' city is ancient Athens, and that his imagined citizens are the real ancestors the priest spoke of. They will match in every way, and it will not be wrong to say they are the very people who lived then. So, each doing his part, they will try their best to do justice to Socrates' topic. Critias asks Socrates to consider whether this story suits him, or whether",
  "greek.tim.26e": "they should look for another. Socrates asks what story could be better. This one is perfect for the Goddess's festival happening now, because it is connected with her, and above all it is true history, not an invented fable. Where else would they find stories if they passed these up? So Critias must give his speech, with good luck, while Socrates, in return for his speech yesterday,",
  "greek.tim.27a": "now keeps quiet and listens. Critias explains the order they have planned for the feast. Since Timaeus is their best astronomer and has made a special study of the nature of the universe, he will speak first, starting with the origin of the cosmos and ending with the birth of humankind. Critias will follow, taking over the human beings Timaeus has brought into being in his speech, and from Socrates",
  "greek.tim.27b": "a select group of very well-educated men. Then, following Solon's law and account, he will bring them before the group as before a court, make them citizens of their city, treating them as the ancient Athenians whom the sacred writings have brought back to light, and from then on speak of them as real citizens of Athens. Socrates says he is being repaid with a generous and splendid feast of speeches, and tells Timaeus it is his turn, once he has called on the gods.",
  "greek.tim.27c": "Timaeus says that everyone with even a little sense calls on God at the start of any task, small or large. So they, who are about to speak about the universe, how it came to be or perhaps never came to be, must call on the gods and goddesses, unless they are quite mad, and pray that everything they say will please them first and themselves second. Let that count as their prayer to the gods.",
  "greek.tim.27d": "They must also call on themselves to proceed so that Socrates can most easily understand and Timaeus can most clearly explain his views. Timaeus says that first they must draw a distinction: what is it that always exists",
  "greek.tim.28a": "and never comes into being, and what is it that is always coming into being and never truly exists? The first can be grasped by thought and reasoning, because it is always the same. The second is grasped by opinion, based on the senses without reasoning, because it comes into being and passes away and never really is. Everything that comes into being must have a cause. And when a maker shapes something while looking at an unchanging model, the result is bound to",
  "greek.tim.28b": "be beautiful; but when he looks at something that has come into being and copies that, the result is not beautiful. Timaeus says they must ask about the whole Heaven, or Cosmos, or whatever name it prefers, the first question to ask about anything: has it always existed with no beginning, or did it come into being from some starting point? It came into being, he says, because it can be seen and touched and has a body, and all such things can be perceived by the senses",
  "greek.tim.28c": "Things we perceive, which we grasp by opinion and the senses, come into being and are produced, and anything that comes into being must have a cause. Timaeus says that finding the Maker and Father of this universe is hard work, and once found, it would be impossible to explain him to everyone. So he returns to the question about the cosmos: which of the two models did its builder copy?",
  "greek.tim.29a": "Did he copy what is unchanging and always the same, or what has come into being? If the cosmos is beautiful and its maker good, Timaeus says, he clearly looked to the eternal; to say otherwise would be impious. And it is obvious that he did, because the cosmos is the most beautiful thing that has come into being and its maker the best of causes. So it was made on the model of what is grasped by reason and thought and never changes.",
  "greek.tim.29b": "If this is granted, Timaeus says, the cosmos must be a copy of something. It is always best to start at the natural beginning. When talking about a copy and its model, the kind of explanation must match the kind of thing being explained. Explanations of what is lasting, firm and grasped by thought will themselves be lasting and unshakeable, and as far as words can be irrefutable,",
  "greek.tim.29c": "they must be. But explanations of the copy, which is only a likeness of the model, will only be likely, matching it: as true being is to becoming, so truth is to belief. So, Timaeus tells Socrates, if in speaking about the gods and the birth of the universe they cannot give accounts that are fully consistent and exact, he should not be surprised. They should be satisfied with an account as likely as any other, remembering that both the speaker",
  "greek.tim.29d": "and his listeners are only human, so they should accept the likely story and not look for more. Socrates says this is excellent and they gladly accept his introduction; now he asks Timaeus to go on to the main subject. Timaeus begins by stating why the one who made it",
  "greek.tim.29e": "made the world of becoming and the universe. He was good, and something good never feels envy about anything. Being free of envy, he wanted everything to be as much like himself as possible. Timaeus says they are entirely right to accept this from wise men as the main starting point of becoming and the cosmos.",
  "greek.tim.30a": "God wanted everything to be good, as far as possible, and nothing bad. So when he took over everything visible and found it not at rest but moving in a jarring, disorderly way, he brought order out of the disorder, thinking order far better. The best being could never do anything but what is finest. Thinking it over, he saw that among things that can be seen by nature,",
  "greek.tim.30b": "nothing without reason would ever be finer, taken as a whole, than something with reason, and that reason cannot exist anywhere except in a soul. So he put reason in soul and soul in body as he built the universe, to make it as fine and good as possible by nature. So, according to the likely account, Timaeus says, this cosmos truly came into being as a living creature with soul and reason, through God's forethought.",
  "greek.tim.30c": "Next, Timaeus asks, which living creature did the maker use as the likeness for the cosmos? Not any creature that is only a part of something, since nothing like an incomplete thing could ever be beautiful. Instead the cosmos most resembles that Living Creature of which all other living creatures, singly and by kind, are parts. That Creature contains all the intelligible living creatures, just as this universe contains us and all the other visible creatures",
  "greek.tim.30d": "that have been made. Because God wanted to make it as like as possible to that intelligible Creature, the most beautiful and complete of all, he made it a single visible living creature, containing within it all the living creatures that are naturally related to it.",
  "greek.tim.31a": "Timaeus asks whether they are right to call the Heaven one, or whether there are many or infinitely many heavens. It must be one, if it is to match its model. The model contains all intelligible living creatures, so it cannot have a second beside it; otherwise there would have to be yet another creature containing both, and the universe would really be modelled on that third one instead.",
  "greek.tim.31b": "So, to match the perfect Living Creature in being unique, its maker made neither two universes nor infinitely many; there is and always will be just this one Heaven. Anything that comes into being must have a body that can be seen and touched. Nothing is visible without fire, nothing tangible without solidity, and nothing solid without earth. So God began making the body of the universe out of fire and earth. But two things cannot be joined well without a third:",
  "greek.tim.31c": "there has to be something in between to bind them. The best bond is one that makes itself and the things it joins as completely one as possible, and proportion does this best by nature. Timaeus explains: when, of any three numbers, cubic or square, the middle one",
  "greek.tim.32a": "is such that the first is to it as it is to the last, and the last is to it as it is to the first, then the middle can become first and last, and the first and last can become middle. So all of them are interchangeable, and being interchangeable they make a unity. Now if the body of the universe",
  "greek.tim.32b": "had been meant to be a flat surface with no depth, one middle term would have been enough to bind it. But it had to be solid, and solids always need two middle terms, not one. So God put water and air between fire and earth, and made them as nearly as possible in the same ratio: air is to water as fire is to air, and water is to earth as air is to water. In this way he joined them and built a Heaven that can be seen and touched.",
  "greek.tim.32c": "So the body of the cosmos was made from these four materials and held in harmony by proportion. This gave it friendship within itself, so that, being united with itself, it could not be undone by anyone except the one who bound it. The cosmos used up the whole of each of the four elements: its maker built it from all the fire, water, air and earth there was, leaving nothing of any of them outside it. He had these aims:",
  "greek.tim.32d": "first, that it should be, as far as possible, a complete and whole living creature with all its parts complete; and next, that it should be one,",
  "greek.tim.33a": "since nothing was left over to make another one like it. He also wanted it to be safe from age and sickness. He saw that when heat, cold and other powerful forces surround a body made of parts and strike it from outside, they break it down before its time and wear it away with sickness and age. So he made it one single whole made of wholes, complete, never ageing and never ill.",
  "greek.tim.33b": "He gave it a suitable shape. A creature meant to contain all living creatures should have the shape that contains all shapes, so he made it round, a sphere, the same distance from the centre to the edge in every direction. This is the most complete shape and the most like itself, and he thought likeness far finer than unlikeness. He made its outer surface perfectly smooth all over, for several reasons.",
  "greek.tim.33c": "It needed no eyes, since there was nothing outside it to see, and no ears, since there was nothing to hear. There was no air around it to breathe, and it needed no organs for taking in food and getting rid of what was left, because nothing came in or went out on any side; there was nothing else. It was designed to feed on its own waste,",
  "greek.tim.33d": "and to do and undergo everything by itself and within itself, because its maker thought it better for it to be self-sufficient than to need anything else. He did not give it useless hands, since it had nothing to grasp or fend off, nor feet, nor any other means of walking.",
  "greek.tim.34a": "For movement, Timaeus says, he gave it the one of the seven motions that suits its body and belongs most to reason and intelligence: he made it spin evenly in one place, turning in a circle within itself. He took away the other six motions and kept it free of their wandering. Since it needed no feet for turning round, he made it without legs or feet. This was the whole plan of the eternal God for the god",
  "greek.tim.34b": "that was going to exist. He made it smooth, even and the same distance from the centre on all sides, a whole and complete body made of complete bodies. In the middle he put Soul, stretched it through the whole, and also wrapped it around the outside. So he set up a single, solitary Heaven, a circle turning in a circle, able because of its goodness to keep itself company, needing no one else, its own acquaintance and friend. For all these reasons he made it a blessed god. Timaeus notes that, although they are describing the Soul after the body,",
  "greek.tim.34c": "God did not plan it to be younger than the body; he would never have let the older be ruled by the younger when he joined them. Humans, Timaeus says, are largely subject to chance, and so is the way they speak. But God made Soul older than Body and first in birth and quality, since she was to be the mistress and ruler and the body the one ruled. He made her from the materials",
  "greek.tim.35a": "and in the way Timaeus now describes. Between the kind of being that is indivisible and always the same, and the kind that is changeable and divided among bodies, he mixed a third kind made from both. He did the same with the Same and the Other, making a middle form of each between its indivisible and its bodily, divided version. Then he took these three and blended them into one, forcing the Other to join with the Same, even though it naturally resists mixing.",
  "greek.tim.35b": "Having mixed them together with Being and made one thing out of three, he divided the whole into the right number of portions, each a mixture of Same, Other and Being. He divided it like this: first one portion; then a portion twice that; then a third portion one and a half times the second, that is, three times the first; a fourth twice the second; a fifth three times the third;",
  "greek.tim.35c": "a sixth eight times the first; and a seventh twenty-seven times the first.",
  "greek.tim.36a": "Next, Timaeus says, he filled the gaps in the series of doubles and triples. He cut more portions from the mixture and put them between the earlier ones, so that each gap held two means: one that exceeds one end and falls short of the other by the same fraction of each end, and one that exceeds and falls short by the same number. These additions created new intervals of 3:2, 4:3 and 9:8, and he then filled the 4:3 intervals with 9:8 intervals.",
  "greek.tim.36b": "This left a small remainder in each case, in the ratio 256:243, and by then the mixture he had been cutting from was used up. Next he split the whole thing lengthwise into two strips and laid them across each other at their middles, like a large cross,",
  "greek.tim.36c": "then bent each into a circle and joined each to itself and to the other at the point opposite where they first crossed. He set them in a motion that turns continually in the same place, making one circle outer and the other inner. The outer motion he named the motion of the Same, the inner the motion of the Other. He made the Same move to the right along the side and the Other to the left along the diagonal, and he gave control",
  "greek.tim.36d": "to the circle of the Same and uniform. He left that one undivided, but split the inner circle in six places into seven unequal circles, according to the double and triple intervals, three of each. He made the two main circles go in opposite directions. Of the seven inner circles, three were to go at the same speed and the other four at different speeds from each other and from the three, but in fixed whole-number ratios. When the Soul was finished to its maker's satisfaction, he built everything bodily inside it,",
  "greek.tim.36e": "and fitted the two together, centre to centre. The Soul, woven all through the Heaven from the centre to the edge and wrapped around it on the outside, turning within herself, began a divine start of endless, intelligent life for all time. The body of the Heaven can be seen, Timaeus says, but the Soul is invisible; she shares in reason and harmony,",
  "greek.tim.37a": "and she is the best of all things that came into being, made by the best of the eternal things that can be known by thought. Because she is a blend of the Same, the Other and Being, divided and bound in proportion, and turns back on herself, whenever she touches anything, whether its being is scattered or undivided, she is stirred through her whole self and declares what that thing is the same as",
  "greek.tim.37b": "and what it is different from, and in what way, where, how and when each thing exists and is affected by others, both among things that change and among things that are always the same. This declaration, equally true about the Other and the Same, moves through the self-moving soul without speech or sound. When it concerns things of the senses, and the circle of the Other, running true, reports it to the whole soul, firm and true opinions and beliefs arise. And when it concerns what reason grasps,",
  "greek.tim.37c": "and the circle of the Same, turning smoothly, reports it, the result is reason and knowledge. Anyone who says these arise in anything other than Soul, Timaeus says, is far from the truth. When the Father who made it saw it moving and alive, a delight to the eternal gods, he was pleased and happy too, and decided to make it even more like its model.",
  "greek.tim.37d": "Since the model is an eternal living creature, he set about making this universe as much like it as he could. But being eternal could not be fully given to something that comes into being. So he planned to make a moving image of eternity. As he ordered the Heaven, he made an everlasting image of eternity, which stays in unity, an image that moves according to number: what we call Time.",
  "greek.tim.37e": "At the same time as he built the Heaven, he arranged for days, nights, months and years, which did not exist before the Heaven. These are all parts of Time, and 'was' and 'will be' are forms of Time that have come into being, though we wrongly and carelessly apply them to eternal being. Timaeus says we say it is, was or will be, when in truth only 'is'",
  "greek.tim.38a": "fits it. 'Was' and 'will be' properly belong to things that come into being over time, because both are kinds of change. What is always unchanging cannot grow older or younger over time, cannot have become so, be so now, or be going to be so, nor be subject to any of the conditions that becoming attaches to things moving in the world of the senses. These are forms of Time, which imitates eternity and circles round by number. We also use expressions like",
  "greek.tim.38b": "'what has happened has happened', 'what is happening is happening', 'what will happen will happen' and 'what does not exist does not exist', but none of these is accurate. Timaeus says now is perhaps not the time to go into this exactly. So Time came into being together with the Heaven, so that, having been made together, they would also come apart together, if that ever happens. It was made on the pattern of the eternal nature, to be as like it as possible: the pattern exists through all eternity,",
  "greek.tim.38c": "while the copy exists through all time, having been, being and going to be. Because of this plan of God's for creating Time, the sun, the moon and five other stars called planets came into being to mark out and keep the numbers of Time. When God had made their bodies, he set them in the orbits along which the circle of the Other moves: seven orbits for the seven bodies.",
  "greek.tim.38d": "He put the Moon in the first circle around the Earth and the Sun in the second. He put the Morning Star and the star sacred to Hermes in circles that move as fast as the Sun but have an opposite pull, which is why the Sun, the star of Hermes and the Morning Star keep catching up with and being caught by one another. As for the rest, to describe where he put them all and why",
  "greek.tim.38e": "would be a bigger job than the main argument it is meant to support, even though it is only a side issue. Perhaps later, when there is time, these points will get the attention they deserve. Timaeus continues: when each of the bodies needed to make Time had reached its own orbit, and they had been born as living creatures with their bodies held by living bonds,",
  "greek.tim.39a": "and had learned their tasks, they went round along the circle of the Other, which lies at a slant across the circle of the Same and is governed by it. Some moved in larger circles and some in smaller; those in smaller circles went faster and those in larger ones slower. Because of the motion of the Same, the fastest seemed to be overtaken by the slowest, though really they were overtaking them.",
  "greek.tim.39b": "The reason is that they move in two opposite directions at once, and the motion of the Same, the fastest of all, twists all their circles into spirals, so the body that falls behind it most slowly seems closest to it. To give a clear measure of their speeds round their eight orbits, God lit a light in the second circle from the earth, which we now call the Sun, so that it would shine as widely as possible through the whole Heaven and the living creatures meant to do so could learn number from the turning of the Same and Similar.",
  "greek.tim.39c": "This is how and why Night and Day came about: they are one turn of the single most intelligent circle. A Month is each time the Moon finishes her orbit and catches up with the Sun; a Year is each time the Sun finishes his orbit. People have not worked out the orbits of the other stars, apart from a few, so they have no names for them and do not measure and compare them, and so they mostly do not realise",
  "greek.tim.39d": "that the wanderings of these bodies, hard to calculate and amazingly complicated, are Time. Still, it can be seen that the full number of Time completes the Perfect Year when all eight orbits, with their different speeds, finish together and arrive back at the starting point, measured by the circle of the Same. This is how and why all the stars that turn as they travel through the Heaven came into being: so that the universe would be as like as possible to the perfect, intelligible Living Creature in imitating its eternal",
  "greek.tim.39e": "nature. With the birth of Time, the world was already like its model in every other way, but it did not yet contain every kind of living creature, so in that it was still unlike. So he finished this remaining part, shaping it after the model. He decided that this world should contain the same kinds, and as many, as reason sees in the perfect Living Creature. Timaeus says there are four kinds: first the heavenly race of gods;",
  "greek.tim.40a": "second, winged creatures that travel through the air; third, creatures that live in water; and fourth, those that walk on land. He made the divine kind mostly of fire, so it would be as bright and beautiful to see as possible. He made it perfectly round, like the universe, and set it in the mind of the highest circle to move along with it, spreading it all round the Heaven as a true ornament worked over the whole. He gave each of them two motions: one is turning evenly in the same place, so it always thinks the same thoughts about the same things,",
  "greek.tim.40b": "and the other is a forward motion, because it is governed by the turning of the Same and Similar. In the other five motions they stay still, so each can be as perfect as possible. That is how all the fixed stars came into being, divine and everlasting living creatures that keep turning evenly in the same place forever; the ones that wander came about in the way described before. And the Earth, our nurse, which is wound around the axis that runs through the whole universe,",
  "greek.tim.40c": "he made to be the guardian and maker of night and day, the first and oldest of the gods born within the Heaven. But the dances of these stars, how they cross each other, how their orbits turn back and move forward, which gods line up together and how many stand opposite, which pass in front of which and when, hiding from our view, and how they reappear",
  "greek.tim.40d": "and send frightening signs of the future to people who cannot calculate: to describe all this without looking at models of their movements would be wasted effort. So, Timaeus says, this is enough about the visible gods who came into being. As for the other gods, finding and explaining their origin is too much for them; they must trust those who told it in the past, who claimed to be descended from gods and must surely know their own ancestors.",
  "greek.tim.40e": "It is impossible, Timaeus says, not to believe the children of gods, even if their claims lack likely or certain proof; since they say they are talking about their own family, we should follow custom and believe them. So, following them, Timaeus gives the gods' family line: Ge and Uranus had Oceanus and Tethys; they had Phorkys, Cronos, Rhea and the others with them;",
  "greek.tim.41a": "Cronos and Rhea had Zeus and Hera and all those known as their brothers and sisters; and these had more descendants. When all the gods had been born, both those that visibly circle the sky and those that show themselves only when they wish, the maker of the universe spoke to them: 'Gods born of gods, the works I have made and fathered cannot be undone unless I will it. Everything bound can be unbound,",
  "greek.tim.41b": "but only a wicked being would wish to undo something well made and in good order. So you, having been born, are not completely immortal or unbreakable, but you will never be undone or die, because my will is a stronger and more powerful bond than the ones that held you together at birth. Now learn what I tell you. Three mortal kinds have not yet been made. Without them the Heaven will be incomplete, because it will not contain every kind of living creature, and it must if",
  "greek.tim.41c": "it is to be fully complete. But if I made them and gave them life, they would be equal to gods. So that they may be mortal and this universe truly be all, you must turn, as nature directs, to making these creatures, copying the power I used in making you. The part of them that deserves to be called immortal, the divine part that rules in those willing to follow justice and you, I will sow and start myself and hand over to you.",
  "greek.tim.41d": "For the rest, weave the mortal together with the immortal, make and bring forth living creatures, feed them so they grow, and take them back when they die.' After saying this, he poured what was left of the earlier material into the same bowl where he had mixed the Soul of the Universe and mixed it in much the same way, but no longer as pure: only second- and third-grade. When it was all mixed, he divided it into as many souls as there are stars and gave each soul a star.",
  "greek.tim.41e": "He set each one, as if in a chariot, showed them the nature of the universe and told them the laws of fate. Their first birth would be the same for all, so that none would be treated unfairly by him. Once each was sown into its own instrument of time, they were to grow into the most god-fearing of living creatures;",
  "greek.tim.42a": "and since human nature has two sexes, the stronger would be the one later called man. When, by necessity, they were planted in bodies, with things constantly flowing into and out of those bodies, certain things would inevitably follow: first, sensation, the same in everyone, arising from violent impressions; second, desire mixed with pleasure and pain; and also fear and anger",
  "greek.tim.42b": "and all the feelings related to them, and their opposites. If they master these, they will live justly; if mastered by them, unjustly. Whoever lives his time well will go back to his home in his own star and have a happy life suited to him. Whoever fails will be reborn the second time as a woman, and if even then he does not stop doing wrong,",
  "greek.tim.42c": "he will be changed each time into some animal whose nature matches the kind of wrong he does. His changes and troubles will not end until he follows the turning of the Same and Similar within him and, by the power of reason, masters the heavy mass of fire, water,",
  "greek.tim.42d": "earth and air that later clung to him, a noisy, unreasoning mass, and so returns to his first and best state. When he had explained all these rules to them, so that he would not be to blame for any of them doing wrong later, he sowed them, some in the Earth, some in the Moon and others in the other instruments of Time. After this sowing he handed over to the young gods the work of shaping mortal bodies and of making and controlling the rest of the human soul that still had to be added, with everything that went with it,",
  "greek.tim.42e": "and of governing this mortal creature as well and as wisely as they could, except so far as it brought evils on itself. Having given all these orders, he stayed in his own usual state. While he did so, Timaeus says, his children listened to their Father's orders and obeyed. They took the immortal starting point of the mortal creature and, copying their own maker, borrowed portions of fire, earth, water and air from the cosmos,",
  "greek.tim.43a": "as if they meant to pay them back, and stuck them together. But they did not use the unbreakable bonds that held them together; they used many pegs too small to see. So they built each body, and inside these bodies, with things constantly flowing in and out, they bound the circles of the immortal Soul. Bound inside a mighty river like this, the souls neither controlled it nor were controlled by it, but were violently tossed along, tossing it too,",
  "greek.tim.43b": "so the whole creature moved, but at random, in a disorderly and unreasoning way, using all six motions: it went forwards and backwards, right and left, up and down, wandering in every direction. The flood of food pouring in and flowing out was huge, but even greater was the turmoil",
  "greek.tim.43c": "inside each creature caused by collisions: when its body struck fire from outside, or a hard lump of earth, or flowing water, or was caught in a storm of wind, and the motions from all these rushed through the body and hit the Soul. That is why all these motions were then called Sensations, as they still are. And since at that time they were causing constant, widespread movement, joining the never-ending stream",
  "greek.tim.43d": "in shaking the Soul's circles violently, they completely blocked the circle of the Same by flowing against it, stopping it from ruling and moving. They also shook the circle of the Other so badly that, in the three double and three triple intervals and the linking ratios of 3:2, 4:3 and 9:8, which only the one who bound them can fully undo, they caused all kinds of twisting, and",
  "greek.tim.43e": "broke and disrupted the circles in every possible way. As a result, barely holding together, they did move, but irrationally, sometimes backwards, sometimes sideways, sometimes upside down. Timaeus gives an example: if a man stands on his head, with his head on the ground and his feet against something above, then to the people watching, his right seems to be on the left and his left on the right, and theirs seem the same way to him. The Soul's circles go through exactly this sort of thing, and strongly;",
  "greek.tim.44a": "whenever they meet something outside, whether of the Same or the Other, they call it the same as something or different from something, getting it wrong, and so they are false and foolish, with no circle in charge to guide them. When outside sensations crash into these circles and sweep the whole vessel of the Soul along with them, the circles seem to be in control when in fact they are being controlled. Because of all this, now just as at the beginning,",
  "greek.tim.44b": "whenever the Soul is bound into a mortal body it is at first without reason. But once the stream of growth and food flows in less strongly, the circles calm down, follow their own path and grow steadier over time. Then, as each circle moves along its natural track, they straighten out and judge Same and Other correctly, and so make their owner reasonable. And if",
  "greek.tim.44c": "this is strengthened by proper education, the person becomes fully healthy and sound, having escaped the worst illness; but if he neglects it completely, he lives a crippled life and goes back to Hades incomplete and foolish. But that happens later, Timaeus says. On the present subject they must be more exact, and about what comes before it, how the parts of the body came into being and the causes and divine plans by which the Soul came to exist, they must stick to the most likely account,",
  "greek.tim.44d": "and go on from there. The gods bound the two divine circles inside a round body, copying the round shape of the universe. We now call it the head; it is the most divine part and rules over everything else in us. The gods gave it the whole body they had put together as its servant, intending that it should share in all the movements there would be.",
  "greek.tim.44e": "So that the head would not roll along the ground, with all its bumps and hollows, unable to climb over the one or out of the other, they gave it the body to carry it about. That, Timaeus says, is why the body was made long and, by God's design, grew four limbs that could stretch and bend,",
  "greek.tim.45a": "to carry it around. Gripping and standing on these, it could travel everywhere, carrying high the home of our most divine and sacred part. That is how and why everyone has legs and hands. And since the gods think the front more honourable than the back, they made us move mostly forwards, so a person's front had to be different from the back. So, starting with the head, they put the face on the front",
  "greek.tim.45b": "and fitted it with organs for all the soul's foresight, and made this natural front the leading part. The first organs they made were the light-bearing eyes, which they set in the face for this reason. They arranged that the kind of fire that does not burn but gives gentle light should form a body like everyday daylight. They made the pure fire inside us, which is related to daylight, flow out through the eyes in a smooth, dense stream;",
  "greek.tim.45c": "and they made the eyes, especially their centres, close-packed, so that they kept back any coarser fire and let only this pure kind through. So when daylight surrounds the stream of sight, like meets like, and the two join into a single body in a straight line from the eyes, wherever the fire streaming out meets an object outside. This body, all alike because of its like nature,",
  "greek.tim.45d": "passes on the movements of whatever it touches, or whatever touches it, through the whole body to the soul, and causes the sensation we call seeing. But when the related fire outside disappears at night, the inner fire is cut off. Going out into something unlike itself, it changes and is put out, since the air next to it no longer has fire in it. So it stops seeing and also makes us sleepy. For the eyelids, which the gods designed",
  "greek.tim.45e": "to protect sight, hold in the inner fire when they close. This calms and settles the movements inside, and when they settle, quiet follows; when the quiet is deep, we fall into an almost dreamless sleep. But when some stronger movements remain, Timaeus says, depending on their nature and where they are,",
  "greek.tim.46a": "they produce images of matching kind and strength, which are copied inside us and remembered when the sleepers wake from the dream. It is now easy, Timaeus says, to see how images form in mirrors and in any bright, smooth surface. They come from the inner and outer fires combining whenever they meet on the smooth surface and are bent in various ways;",
  "greek.tim.46b": "all such reflections happen because the fire from the face being reflected joins with the fire of sight on the bright, smooth surface. Left looks like right because opposite parts of the stream of sight touch opposite parts of the object, unlike in a normal meeting. On the other hand, right looks right and left looks left when the fire swaps sides in joining with the object",
  "greek.tim.46c": "it meets, and this happens when the smooth surface of the mirror is curved up on both sides and throws the right part of the sight-stream to the left and the left to the right. And when the same mirror is turned lengthways to the face, it makes the whole face look upside down, throwing the bottom of the ray to the top and the top to the bottom. All of these, Timaeus says, are helper causes that God uses as servants in bringing about, as far as possible,",
  "greek.tim.46d": "the form of the Best. But most people think they are not helpers but the main causes of everything, cooling and heating, hardening and melting and so on. Yet they cannot have reason or thought for any purpose. The only thing that can have thought is Soul, and Soul is invisible, while fire, water, earth and air are all visible bodies. Anyone who loves thought and knowledge must look first",
  "greek.tim.46e": "for the causes that come from intelligent nature, and only second for those belonging to things that are moved by others and, in turn, move others because they have to. They should do the same, Timaeus says: talk about both kinds of cause, but keep apart those that, with thought, make things fine and good, from those that have no intelligence and always produce random, disorderly results. That is enough about the helper causes that gave the eyes their power. Next they must explain the greatest",
  "greek.tim.47a": "benefit the eyes give, the reason God gave them to us. In Timaeus's view, sight is the source of our greatest benefit, because none of what has been said about the universe could ever have been said if people had never seen the stars, the sun or the sky. As it is, seeing day and night, months and the turning years has given us number, the idea of Time, and a way to investigate the nature of the universe. From these we have got philosophy in all its range,",
  "greek.tim.47b": "and the gods have never given, and never will give, a greater gift to mortals. That, he says, is the greatest good of eyesight. Why praise the lesser ones? Someone who is not a philosopher might wail uselessly if he lost his sight. But the real reason for this best gift is this: God invented sight and gave it to us so that we could watch the circling of Reason in the heavens and use it for the circling of the reasoning inside us, which is related to it,",
  "greek.tim.47c": "though ours is easily disturbed and theirs is not. By learning and sharing in calculations that are naturally correct, and copying the god's perfectly steady circles, we could steady the wandering circles inside ourselves. The same goes for sound and hearing, Timaeus says: the gods gave them for the same purpose and reasons. Speech was made for these same ends and contributes most to them, and music, in so far as it uses sound we can hear,",
  "greek.tim.47d": "was given for the sake of harmony. Harmony, whose movements are related to the circles of the soul in us, was given by the Muses to anyone who uses them wisely, not for mindless pleasure, as people now think, but to help the soul's inner circle when it has lost its harmony, bringing it back into order and agreement with itself. And because most of us are",
  "greek.tim.47e": "out of tune and lack grace, the same gods gave us rhythm too, to help us for the same reasons. Timaeus says that what he has said so far, apart from a small part, has been about the works of Reason; but they must also explain what comes about through Necessity.",
  "greek.tim.48a": "In truth, this cosmos came into being as a mixture of Necessity and Reason. Reason ruled Necessity by persuading her to bring most things that came into being towards the best result. So it was through Necessity giving way to wise persuasion that the universe was first put together like this. So anyone who wants to explain how it really came about must include the 'wandering cause' and the way it truly works. So they must go back,",
  "greek.tim.48b": "and start again from a suitable new beginning, just as they did with the earlier subjects. They must look at the real nature of fire, water, air and earth as it was before the Heaven was born, and what they were like then. No one has yet explained how they came about, Timaeus says; people talk as if everyone knows what fire and the others are, calling them first principles and elements of the universe, when really they do not even deserve to be compared,",
  "greek.tim.48c": "by anyone with a grain of sense, to syllables. For now, though, Timaeus will not explain the first principle, or principles, of everything, simply because it is hard to set out his views in the way they are now proceeding. So Socrates should not expect him to, and Timaeus could never persuade himself he would be right to take on such a big task. Sticking, then,",
  "greek.tim.48d": "to what they agreed earlier about giving a likely account, he will try, as before, to give an account as likely as any other, or more so, about both individual things and the whole, right from the start. And as before, at the beginning of the account they must call on God the Saviour to bring them safely through an unusual and unfamiliar explanation",
  "greek.tim.48e": "to a likely conclusion, and so begin again. But in starting this new account of the universe, Timaeus says, they need more distinctions than before. Then they distinguished two kinds; now they must name a third. Two were enough for the earlier account: one was the model, grasped by thought and always the same,",
  "greek.tim.49a": "and the second was the copy of the model, which comes into being and can be seen. They did not pick out a third kind then, thinking two enough, but now the argument seems to force them to try to describe in words a kind that is puzzling and obscure. What is its main property? Above all, it is the receptacle, a kind of nurse, of everything that comes into being. That is true, Timaeus says, but they must describe it more clearly.",
  "greek.tim.49b": "That is hard, especially because they first have to deal with the problem of fire and the other elements. With these, it is hard to say which one should really be called water rather than fire, or which should be called any one of them rather than all of them, while still using words that are reliable and fixed. So how can they handle this, and what likely answer can they give? First, they see what we now call water becoming, by thickening,",
  "greek.tim.49c": "stones and earth, as we believe; and the same thing, by melting and spreading out, becoming wind and air; and air, by burning, becoming fire; and fire, when squeezed and put out, going back to being air, and air again gathering and thickening into cloud and mist, and from these, squeezed further, flowing water; and from water, earth and stones again. So the elements seem to hand on to one another,",
  "greek.tim.49d": "in an endless circle, the gift of birth. Since none of them ever keeps the same look, which of them can anyone firmly say is this element and not another without being laughed at? None. The safest way to talk about them is this: whatever we see always changing from one state to another, like fire, we should never call 'this' but 'such', and the same with water and every other element, never speaking as if it were stable,",
  "greek.tim.49e": "the way we do with words like 'this' and 'that', which we think point to a definite thing. Such things slip away from 'this' and 'that' and any name that suggests they are fixed. So we should not call the elements 'these', but for each of them and all together use 'such', for what is always going round. So we should call what is always 'such' by the name 'fire', and so with everything else that comes into being. But the thing in which they always seem",
  "greek.tim.50a": "to appear, one after another, and from which they then disappear: only that should be called 'this' or 'that'. Things that are 'such', like hot or white or their opposites or any mix of them, should never be called by those words. Timaeus tries to explain this more clearly. Imagine someone shaping every possible figure out of gold and constantly reshaping each into the others. If someone pointed to one figure and asked what it was,",
  "greek.tim.50b": "the safest true answer would be 'gold'. One should never say the triangle or any other figure formed in it 'is', since it changes even while being named; one should be happy if it can safely be called 'such'. And the same must be said of the substance that receives all bodies.",
  "greek.tim.50c": "It must always be called by the same name, because it never loses its own nature. Though it receives everything, it never in any way takes on the shape of anything that enters it. By nature it is like modelling material for everything, moved and marked by the shapes that enter it, and because of them it looks different at different times. The shapes that come and go are copies of the things that always exist, stamped from them in a wonderful way that is hard to describe, which they will look at later. For now, Timaeus says, they must think of three kinds: what comes into being, that in which it comes into being, and the source from which what comes into being",
  "greek.tim.50d": "is copied. It is fitting to compare the receiver to a mother, the source to a father, and what comes between them to their child. And they should see that if the stamped copy is to take on all kinds of different looks, the material it is stamped into can only do its job if it has none of those forms itself.",
  "greek.tim.50e": "If it were like any of the forms coming in, then when forms of an opposite or quite different kind arrived, it would copy them badly, because its own shape would show through. So the substance that is to receive all kinds must have no form of its own. It is like perfume-makers, who skilfully make the liquids that will take the scents as scent-free as possible; and people who mould shapes in soft material refuse to let any earlier shape show and first make it even and as smooth as they can.",
  "greek.tim.51a": "In the same way, Timaeus says, the substance that is to receive, all over and many times, copies of everything intelligible and eternal must have no forms of its own. So they should not call the Mother and Receptacle of this visible, perceptible world earth, air, fire or water, or anything made of or from them. If they call her an invisible, shapeless kind that receives everything and in some very puzzling way shares in the intelligible,",
  "greek.tim.51b": "they will describe her truly. As far as one can tell from what has been said, the best way to put it is this: the part of her that is made fiery looks like fire each time, the part made liquid looks like water, and she looks like earth and air in so far as she takes on copies of them. But Timaeus wants to look at this more exactly and asks: is there such a thing as fire in itself,",
  "greek.tim.51c": "or any of the other things we call things in themselves? Or do only the things we see or otherwise sense with the body exist, so that nothing else exists anywhere in any way, and it is just empty talk to say there is always an intelligible Form of each thing, when it is really only a phrase? It would not be right, he says, to dismiss the question untried and simply declare the answer; but they also should not weigh down a long speech with another side argument.",
  "greek.tim.51d": "It would be best, Timaeus says, if they could briefly lay down some main deciding principle. So he gives his vote. If understanding (Reason) and true opinion are two different things, then these Forms in themselves certainly exist, which we cannot sense and can grasp only by reason. But if, as some think, true opinion is no different from reason, then everything we perceive with our bodily senses must be counted as the most solid reality.",
  "greek.tim.51e": "But the two must be called two, because they arise separately and are different in nature. One comes to us through teaching, the other through persuasion. One always comes with true reasoning, the other has no reasoning. One cannot be changed by persuasion, the other can. And everyone has true opinion, but only the gods and a few people have Reason. That being so, Timaeus says, they must agree that one kind",
  "greek.tim.52a": "is the Form that is always the same, never born and never destroyed, never taking anything else into itself from anywhere nor going into anything else, invisible and unable to be sensed in any way: this is what Reason studies. A second kind has the same name and is like it, but can be sensed, is born, is always moving about, comes into being in a place and then perishes from it, and is grasped by opinion together with the senses. A third kind is Place, which always exists,",
  "greek.tim.52b": "cannot be destroyed, and gives room to everything that is born. It is grasped without the senses by a sort of 'bastard reasoning', and is hardly believable. When we look at it, we dream, as it were, and say that everything that exists must be somewhere, taking up some space, and that whatever is neither on earth nor anywhere in heaven is nothing. Because of these and related ideas, when we wake up we cannot clearly tell apart the truly real, sleepless being, because of our dreamy state,",
  "greek.tim.52c": "or state the truth: that a copy does not even own the thing it was made to represent, but is always a passing ghost of something else, and so must come to be in something else, clinging to existence as best it can, or else be nothing at all. But what truly exists is backed by the precise, true argument that, as long as one thing is one thing and another is something different, neither will ever come to be in the other so that the same thing is at once",
  "greek.tim.52d": "one and two. So, Timaeus says, this is his reasoned verdict in brief: Being, Place and Becoming existed as three separate things even before the Heaven came into being. And the Nurse of Becoming, turned to liquid and to fire, taking on the forms of earth and air, and going through everything else that comes with them,",
  "greek.tim.52e": "shows every kind of appearance. But because she is filled with powers that are neither alike nor balanced, no part of her is in balance; she sways unevenly everywhere, is shaken by these forms and shakes them in turn as she moves. As they are moved, the forms keep flying off in different directions and getting sorted, just as grain shaken in sieves and other cleaning tools sends the solid, heavy bits to one place",
  "greek.tim.53a": "and the light, fluffy bits flying off to settle somewhere else. So it was with the four kinds shaken by the Receptacle: her movement, like a shaking tool, pushed the unlike kinds furthest apart and the like ones closest together. That is why these kinds were in different places even before the universe was ordered and made out of them. Before then, everything was without reason or measure. But when the work of ordering the universe began,",
  "greek.tim.53b": "fire, water, earth and air, though they had some traces of their own nature, were in the state anything is likely to be in when God is absent. Since that was their natural state then, God began by giving them shape with forms and numbers. Above all, Timaeus says, their account must always assume that God made them as beautiful and good as he could, when they had not been so. Now he must try to explain the arrangement and origin",
  "greek.tim.53c": "of each of these kinds, in an unusual sort of explanation; but since his listeners know something of the technical method he must use, they will follow. First, it is obvious to everyone that fire, earth, water and air are solid bodies, and every body has depth. Depth must be bounded by flat surfaces, and a flat surface with straight edges is made of triangles.",
  "greek.tim.53d": "All triangles come from two kinds of triangle, each with one right angle and two sharp ones. One kind has half a right angle on each side, with two equal sides; the other has the right angle split into unequal parts, with unequal sides. Timaeus takes these as the starting points of fire and the other bodies, using a method that mixes the likely with the necessary. The principles higher than these are known only to God and to anyone God loves.",
  "greek.tim.53e": "Next they must say what the four most beautiful bodies would be: different from one another, but some able to turn into others by breaking up. If they manage this, they will grasp the truth about how earth and fire and the in-between elements came to be. No one will convince them that there are more beautiful bodies, each of its kind, anywhere. So they must work hard to put together these four beautiful kinds of body and to say they have understood",
  "greek.tim.54a": "their nature well enough. Timaeus says the isosceles triangle comes in only one form, but the scalene in endless forms, and they must choose the most beautiful of those if they are to start well. If anyone can show he has chosen a more beautiful one for building these bodies, he wins, as a friend, not an enemy. But Timaeus will skip the rest and take as the most beautiful the triangle of which two, put together,",
  "greek.tim.54b": "make an equilateral triangle. Why would take longer to explain, he says, but if anyone proves him wrong, he is welcome to the prize. So they choose these two triangles to build fire and the other elements: the isosceles, and the one whose longer side squared is always three times its shorter side squared. Now he must clear up something said vaguely earlier. It looked as though the four kinds,",
  "greek.tim.54c": "as they came into being, all turned into one another, but that was misleading. From the chosen triangles, four kinds are made: three from the triangle with unequal sides, and only the fourth from the isosceles. So they cannot all break down into one another, making a few large bodies from many small ones or the reverse; only three of them can. Those three are all made from the same triangle, so when the larger bodies break up, many small ones will form from the same pieces, taking their proper shapes;",
  "greek.tim.54d": "and when many small bodies are broken down into their triangles, those can join into one large body of another kind. That, Timaeus says, is how they turn into each other. Next he explains the shape of each kind and the numbers it is made from. First comes the basic shape with the smallest parts, whose element is the triangle whose longest side is twice its shortest. When two of these are joined along their longest sides, and this is done three times, with the long sides",
  "greek.tim.54e": "and short sides meeting at a centre, those six triangles make one equilateral triangle. When four equilateral triangles are put together so that three of their angles",
  "greek.tim.55a": "meet at a point, they make one solid corner, just less than the widest flat angle. When four such corners are made, the first solid figure is built, which divides the sphere around it into equal, similar parts. The second solid is made from the same triangles, but from eight equilateral triangles, with four flat angles meeting at each corner; when six such corners are made, the second body is complete.",
  "greek.tim.55b": "The third solid is made of 120 of the basic triangles joined together, with twelve corners, each formed by five equilateral triangles, and it has twenty equilateral triangular faces. The first basic triangle stopped once it had made these three solids; the fourth kind was made from the isosceles triangle. Four of these, with their right angles meeting at the centre, made one square, and six squares",
  "greek.tim.55c": "joined together made eight corners, each formed by three right angles. The body built like this was a cube, with six square faces. There was still one more figure, the fifth, and God used it for the universe, decorating it. Thinking about all this, Timaeus says, someone might wonder whether to say there are endlessly many universes or a limited number. If he thought it through, he would decide that believing in endlessly many is the view of someone ignorant",
  "greek.tim.55d": "of things he ought to know. Whether there is really one universe or five is a question that might more reasonably make them pause. Their view, on the likely account, is that there is one, but someone else, looking at other things, will think differently; they must leave him be. Now they should assign the kinds their argument has produced to fire, earth, water and air. To earth they give the cube, because earth is the least movable of the four",
  "greek.tim.55e": "and the most easily shaped, and the body with the steadiest bases must be like that. Of the triangles they started with, the one with equal sides has a naturally steadier base than the one with unequal sides; and of the flat shapes built from them, the square, in its parts and as a whole, has a steadier base than the equilateral triangle.",
  "greek.tim.56a": "So, Timaeus says, they keep to the likely account by giving this shape to earth; the least movable of the remaining shapes to water, the most movable to fire, and the one in between to air. Likewise the smallest body goes to fire, the largest to water and the middle one to air; and the sharpest to fire, the next to air, and the third to water. Of all these shapes, the one with the fewest faces must be the most movable,",
  "greek.tim.56b": "since it is the sharpest and most pointed in every way; and it must be the lightest, since it is made of the fewest identical parts. The second shape comes second in these qualities and the third third. So, on the correct and likely account, the solid shaped as a pyramid is the element and seed of fire; the second is air and the third is water. All of these must be imagined as so small that none of them,",
  "greek.tim.56c": "on its own, can be seen by us; only when many gather together can we see the mass. And as for the number-ratios behind their sizes, movements and other qualities, we must think that God worked them out exactly everywhere, as far as Necessity allowed, willingly or by persuasion, and so arranged everything in harmonious proportion. From all this, these kinds",
  "greek.tim.56d": "will most likely behave like this. When earth meets fire and is broken up by its sharpness, it will keep moving, whether it is broken up in fire itself or in a mass of air or water, until its particles meet somewhere and join together again and become earth, because earth never changes into another form. But water, broken up by fire or by air, can become one particle of fire and two of air;",
  "greek.tim.56e": "and the pieces of one broken-up particle of air will make two particles of fire. And when a little fire is shut in by a lot of air, water or earth, moving among them as they rush about, and loses the struggle and breaks up, two particles of fire join to make one of air. And when air is beaten and broken up, two and a half particles of air make one of water.",
  "greek.tim.57a": "Timaeus looks at it another way. When any other kind is caught in fire, it is cut up by fire's sharp angles and edges; but once it has been remade into fire, it stops being cut, because a kind that is alike and uniform cannot change, or be affected by, another in the same uniform state. But while it is changing into another form, as a weaker body fighting a stronger, it keeps being broken up. And again,",
  "greek.tim.57b": "when a few smaller particles are caught among many larger ones and are broken and put out, if they agree to be remade in the shape of the winning kind, they stop being put out, and air comes from fire and water from air. But if they resist joining these or any other kinds, they keep breaking up until either they are pushed out to their own kind or they are beaten and become one form like the winner, living with it as one family. It is because of these processes",
  "greek.tim.57c": "that they all swap places. The bulk of each kind keeps to its own region because of the Receptacle's movement, but particles that become unlike themselves and like others are carried by the shaking towards the region of the kind they now resemble. These are the causes of how all the pure, basic bodies came into being. But inside the four kinds there are other varieties, and the cause is in how each of the two basic triangles was made: each one originally came",
  "greek.tim.57d": "not in just one size but in larger and smaller sizes, as many as there are varieties within the kinds. So when they combine with themselves and each other, the variety is endless, and anyone who wants to reason likely about nature must keep this in mind. Unless they can agree about motion and rest, how and when they happen,",
  "greek.tim.57e": "the rest of their argument will be badly held up. Some of this has been said already, but they must add that motion never exists where things are uniform. It is hard, or rather impossible, for something to be moved without something to move it, or for something to move without something to be moved; without these there is no motion, and they can never be uniform. So, Timaeus says, rest always goes with uniformity,",
  "greek.tim.58a": "and motion with lack of uniformity, and lack of uniformity is caused by inequality. They have explained where inequality comes from, but not why these bodies do not settle apart into their separate kinds and stop moving through each other. So he explains it again. The turning of the whole universe holds all the kinds and squeezes them all together, because it is round and naturally tends to draw in on itself, so it lets no empty space remain.",
  "greek.tim.58b": "That is why fire has got into everything most of all, and air second, being second in fineness, and so on with the rest. Those with the largest parts leave the most empty space in their structure, and those with the smallest the least. So the squeezing forces the small bodies into the gaps between the large ones. With small bodies next to large, the small breaking up the large and the large binding the small together, they all keep shifting up and down",
  "greek.tim.58c": "towards their own regions, because when their sizes change, their places change too. Since in this way and for these reasons lack of uniformity is constantly kept up, it keeps these bodies moving forever, now and in the future. Next, Timaeus says, there are many kinds of fire: for example, flame; the kind that comes from flame and gives light to the eyes without burning; and the kind left in the embers after the flame goes out.",
  "greek.tim.58d": "Air likewise has kinds: the clearest, called aether; the murkiest, mist and darkness; and other kinds with no name, which come from the triangles being of unequal sizes. Water comes mainly in two kinds: liquid and meltable. The liquid kind has small water particles of unequal size, so it moves easily, by itself and when pushed, because it is uneven and because of its shape. The other kind is made of large",
  "greek.tim.58e": "and uniform particles, so it is steadier than the first and heavy, made solid by its uniformity. But when fire gets in and breaks it up, it loses its uniformity and moves more; once it can move, the air next to it pushes it and it spreads over the ground. Each of these changes has its own name: the breaking up of its masses is called melting, and its spreading over the ground is called flowing. Again, Timaeus says, since the fire leaving the water",
  "greek.tim.59a": "does not go into empty space but presses on the air next to it, the air in turn pushes the still-runny liquid into the places the fire has left and joins it to itself. The mass, squeezed like this and getting back its uniformity now that the fire, which made it uneven, has gone, returns to being itself. The fire's departure is called cooling, and the coming together that follows is called freezing (solidifying).",
  "greek.tim.59b": "Of all the meltable kinds of water, the densest is made of the finest and most uniform particles: a single special kind, shining and yellow, the most precious of possessions, gold, which has filtered through rock and hardened. Gold's offshoot, very hard because it is so dense and black in colour, is called adamant. Another kind has particles close to gold's but of more than one form; it is denser than gold and contains small, fine bits of earth, so it is harder, but it is also lighter",
  "greek.tim.59c": "because it has large gaps inside it. This kind of bright, solid water, made like this, is called bronze. The earth mixed into it separates out on its own when the two grow old and part, and then it is called rust. It is no longer hard, Timaeus says, to explain the rest of these things fully, if one aims at a likely description. When someone, for a break, puts aside arguments about eternal realities and thinks about likely accounts of things that come into being,",
  "greek.tim.59d": "he gets a pleasure he will not regret and gives himself a pastime that is moderate and sensible. So they will now indulge in this pastime and set out the next likely points about these things in order. Water mixed with fire, which is fine and runny, is called fluid because of how it moves and rolls over the ground. It is also soft, because its bases are less stable than earth's and give way. When this kind is separated from fire and air and left on its own, it becomes more uniform,",
  "greek.tim.59e": "but as they flow out it is squeezed in on itself. When it hardens like this, the part above the ground most affected is called hail, and the part on the ground ice; the part less affected and only half-solid is called snow when above the ground, and hoar-frost when it forms on the ground from dew. Timaeus says that most kinds of water mixed with each other, taken as a whole, made of water filtered through plants that grow from the earth,",
  "greek.tim.60a": "are called juices. Because the various sorts have become different through mixing, most of them have no names. But four kinds, which are fiery and especially noticeable, have been named. The one that warms the soul as well as the body is wine. The one that is smooth and divides the sight, and so looks bright, gleaming and shiny, is oil, including pitch, castor oil, olive oil and all others like them;",
  "greek.tim.60b": "the kind that relaxes the tightened parts of the mouth as far as it can, and so gives sweetness, is generally called honey; and the frothy kind that eats away flesh by burning, and is found in all the juices, is called verjuice (sour juice). Of the kinds of earth, the kind filtered through water becomes stone like this. When the water mixed with it is broken up in the mixing, it turns into air, and as air it rushes up to its own region;",
  "greek.tim.60c": "but because there is no empty space above, it pushes against the air next to it. That air, being heavy, is pushed and poured around the mass of earth, crushing it hard and squeezing it into the spaces the new air is leaving. Earth squeezed by air so that water cannot dissolve it becomes stone. The finer kind is made of equal, uniform parts and is see-through; the coarser kind is the opposite. The kind from which all the moisture has been swept away by fast fire, and which is more brittle",
  "greek.tim.60d": "than the first, is what we call earthenware (pottery). Sometimes, when moisture is still left in the earth, and it has been melted by fire and cooled again, it becomes the black kind of stone. There are also two kinds that, in exactly the same way, lose much of their water after mixing, but are made of finer bits of earth and are salty. When these become half-solid and can be dissolved by water again, one of them cleans away oil and earth and is called lye; the other, which goes well with combinations that affect the sense of taste,",
  "greek.tim.60e": "is what is usually called 'dear to the gods': salt. As for the kinds that are a mix of these two and can be melted by fire but not by water, Timaeus explains why. Fire and air do not melt lumps of earth, because their particles are smaller than the gaps in earth's structure, so they pass through easily without forcing their way and leave the earth unmelted. But water particles are larger and have to force their way out, so they break up and melt the earth.",
  "greek.tim.61a": "So earth that is not tightly packed is dissolved only by water, and earth that is packed is dissolved only by fire, since nothing else can get in. Water packed most tightly is dissolved only by fire, but less tightly packed water is dissolved by both fire and air, air working through the gaps and fire through the triangles. Air packed tightly is broken up only into its basic triangles, and when not packed it is melted only by fire. As for bodies made of earth and water,",
  "greek.tim.61b": "as long as water fills the gaps in tightly packed earth, water coming from outside cannot get in, but flows around the whole lump and leaves it undissolved. But when bits of fire get into the gaps in the water, they do to the water what water does to earth, so they alone make the mixture dissolve and flow. Of these, those with less water than earth make up the whole kind called glass,",
  "greek.tim.61c": "and all the meltable stones; those with more water include all the hardened substances like wax and frankincense. Timaeus says they have now explained fairly fully the four kinds, with all their varied shapes, mixtures and changes; but they still need to explain the causes of the way they affect us. First, the things discussed must always be perceptible by the senses; but they have not yet described how flesh and what belongs to it came to be, nor the mortal part of the soul. These cannot be properly explained",
  "greek.tim.61d": "without the sensations, nor the sensations without them, and explaining both at once is hardly possible. So they must assume one to start with and come back to their assumptions later. So that the sensations can follow straight after the kinds, they will take the facts about body and soul as given. First, then, they consider why we call fire hot, by noticing how it acts",
  "greek.tim.61e": "on our bodies, dividing and cutting. We all feel that it is sharp. But the thinness of its edges, the sharpness of its corners, the smallness of its particles and the speed of its motion, which make fire fierce and keen so that it slices whatever it meets, must be explained by remembering",
  "greek.tim.62a": "how its shape came about: more than anything else, it divides our bodies and chops them small, and so naturally produces the feeling we call heat and gives it its name. The opposite feeling is obvious, but still needs describing. When liquids with larger particles around the body get into it, they push out the smaller particles, but because they cannot fit into their place, they squeeze the moisture inside us, so that instead of unevenness and motion they cause stillness and density,",
  "greek.tim.62b": "through uniformity and squeezing. But what is being squeezed against its nature fights back and pushes outward in the other direction. This fighting and shaking we call trembling and shivering, and the whole feeling and its cause are called cold. 'Hard' means everything our flesh gives way to, and 'soft' everything that gives way to our flesh, and the words are used the same way of things compared with each other. Something gives way when its base is small; but what is built",
  "greek.tim.62c": "on square bases is very firmly set and least yielding, as is anything very dense and rigid. Heavy and light, Timaeus says, are best explained along with what are called above and below. People suppose there are two separate, completely opposite regions, each taking half the universe: one called below, where everything with bodily weight moves, and the other above, where things go only against their will.",
  "greek.tim.62d": "That is completely wrong. Since the whole Heaven is round, all its outermost parts are equally far from the centre and equally outermost, and the centre, equally far from all of them, must be thought of as opposite to all of them. Since the cosmos is like this, which of these could anyone call above or below without rightly being accused of using quite the wrong word? The centre is neither above nor below, just the centre; and the outside is not the centre, and no part of it is further from the centre than any other. What opposite names could rightly apply to something completely uniform? Suppose, Timaeus says, a solid body were balanced at the centre of the universe:",
  "greek.tim.63a": "it would never be pulled to the edges, since they are the same in every way. Even if a man walked round it in a circle, he would often call the same spot both above and below, depending on which side he stood. Since the whole is round, as they said, no sensible person would say it has one region above and one below. But where these names come from, and what they really mean, so that we use them even of the whole Heaven,",
  "greek.tim.63b": "they must work out like this. Suppose a man stood in the region of the universe where fire mostly lives and where the greatest mass of what fire moves towards is gathered. Suppose he could separate bits of fire and weigh them, putting them on scales, lifting the beam and forcing the fire into the air, which is unlike it. Clearly he would force the smaller amount more easily than the larger.",
  "greek.tim.63c": "If two masses are lifted together by one effort, the smaller will give way more and the larger less, because it resists; and the large one will be called heavy and moving down, the small one light and moving up. This is exactly what we should notice ourselves doing here. Standing on the earth, we pick up earthy things, and sometimes pure earth, and force them into the air, against nature, since both cling to their own kind;",
  "greek.tim.63d": "and the smaller lump gives way more easily and comes first as we force it into the unlike kind. So we call it light, and the place we force it to 'above', and the opposite 'heavy' and 'below'. So these must be different in relation to each other, because the main masses of the kinds lie in opposite regions. If we compare what is light in one region with what is light in the opposite one, heavy with heavy, below with below",
  "greek.tim.63e": "and above with above, we will find they all turn out opposite, slanting and different in every way. But one thing holds for all of them: a thing's movement towards its own kind makes it heavy, and the place it moves to is 'below'; the opposite conditions give the opposite results. That is their account of these causes. As for smooth and rough, Timaeus says, anyone could see and explain the causes: roughness comes from hardness plus unevenness, and smoothness",
  "greek.tim.64a": "from evenness plus density. As for the feelings the whole body shares, an important question still remains: what causes the pleasures and pains that go with the sensations they have discussed, and with those sensations that come through particular parts of the body and bring pain and pleasure with them. Timaeus proposes to find the causes of every feeling, noticed or not, in the following way,",
  "greek.tim.64b": "remembering the earlier distinction between things that move easily and things that do not; this is how they must track down everything they want to understand. When something that naturally moves easily receives even a small impression, it passes it round, the particles handing on the same impression to each other until it reaches the thinking part and reports what caused it. But something of the opposite kind, being stable and not passing things round, is affected only in itself and does not move the particles next to it. So,",
  "greek.tim.64c": "since its particles do not pass on the first impression to each other, it does not affect the creature as a whole, and the affected part feels nothing. That is true of bones, hair and our other mostly earthy parts. The first kind is true above all of the organs of sight and hearing, because they contain a great deal of fire and air. Now, Timaeus says, this is how they should think of pleasure and pain.",
  "greek.tim.64d": "When something violent and against nature happens in us strongly, it hurts; the return to our natural state, when it is strong, is pleasant. A mild, gradual change is not noticed, and the opposite is the reverse. Something that happens entirely easily is very noticeable but brings no pain or pleasure; for example, what happens to the stream of sight itself, which, as said before, becomes in daylight a body one with ours. It feels no pain when cut or burned",
  "greek.tim.64e": "or anything else, and no pleasure when it goes back to its first state; but it has the strongest and clearest perception of everything that affects it, and of everything it strikes or touches, because there is no force at all in its spreading out or drawing in. But bodies made of larger particles, which give way only with difficulty and pass their motions to the whole, feel pleasures and pains:",
  "greek.tim.65a": "pain when they are changed, and pleasure when they are restored to their first state. Parts that lose and empty slowly but are refilled strongly and plentifully do not feel the emptying but do feel the refilling. So they give no pain to the mortal part of the soul, only the greatest pleasures, as is clear with sweet scents. But parts that are changed violently and restored slowly and with difficulty",
  "greek.tim.65b": "to their first state give the opposite result, as is clearly the case with burns and cuts to the body. Timaeus says they have now given a fairly complete account of the feelings shared by the whole body and the names of what causes them. Next they must try, if they can, to describe what happens in particular parts of the body, both the feelings and the things said to cause them.",
  "greek.tim.65c": "First they must try to explain as well as possible the feelings they skipped earlier when talking about flavours, which belong to the tongue. Clearly these, like most others, come about through tightenings and loosenings, and more than other feelings they also involve roughness and smoothness. All the earthy particles that enter through the small veins, which reach as far as the heart and act",
  "greek.tim.65d": "as the tongue's testing tools, strike the moist, soft parts of the flesh and dissolve, tightening the small veins and drying them out. The rougher ones taste astringent, the less rough ones harsh. Those that act like cleaners on these veins and wash the whole surface of the tongue, when they overdo it and grip the tongue so as to dissolve some of it, as alkalies do,",
  "greek.tim.65e": "are all called bitter. Those milder than alkalies, cleaning only moderately, taste salty to us and more pleasant, without the rough bitterness. And those that take on the warmth of the mouth and are smoothed by it, when they are fully heated and in turn burn the part that heated them, fly upward because they are light towards the senses in the head and cut everything they hit;",
  "greek.tim.66a": "because of this they are all called pungent (sharp). Again, when particles already thinned by rotting enter the narrow veins and match the particles of earth and air there, they make them swirl round each other and ferment. As they ferment they move round into new places and make new hollows that wrap around the incoming particles. In this way, air is wrapped in a moist film,",
  "greek.tim.66b": "sometimes made of earth, sometimes of pure moisture, forming moist, hollow, round pockets of air. The ones made of pure moisture are the clear globes called bubbles, while the earthy kind that stirs and seethes all through is called boiling and fermenting; and what causes this is called acid. The opposite of all these feelings",
  "greek.tim.66c": "comes from the opposite condition. When the particles entering the moist parts are naturally suited to the state of the tongue, they oil and smooth its roughened parts, tightening what is unnaturally loosened and loosening what is tightened, settling everything as far as possible into its natural state. Every such relief from a forced condition is pleasant and welcome to everyone, and is called sweet.",
  "greek.tim.66d": "That is enough on this subject, Timaeus says. Next, the sense of smell in the nostrils has no fixed kinds. Smells as a whole are only half-formed, and no basic kind has the right proportions to carry a smell. The veins in the nose are too narrow for earth and water and too wide for fire and air, so no one has ever smelled any of these themselves, only things that are getting wet, rotting, melting or turning to vapour.",
  "greek.tim.66e": "Smells arise in the in-between state, when water is turning into air or air into water, and they are all smoke or mist: air turning to water is mist, water turning to air is smoke. That is why all smells are thinner than water and thicker than air. This is clear, Timaeus says, when something blocks the breathing and a person breathes in hard: then no smell comes through with it, only the breath on its own. So the kinds of smell have no names,",
  "greek.tim.67a": "since they do not come from many or simple forms; they are only called by two words, pleasant and unpleasant. The unpleasant kind roughens and disturbs the whole hollow of the body between the head and the navel, while the pleasant kind soothes the same area and gently brings it back to its natural state. The third sense organ to describe, Timaeus says, is hearing,",
  "greek.tim.67b": "and what causes its sensations. In general, sound is a blow passed through the ears by the air to the brain and the blood and on to the soul. The movement this causes, starting in the head and ending around the liver, is hearing. Fast movement makes a high sound, slower movement a deeper sound; steady movement makes an even, smooth sound and the opposite a harsh one;",
  "greek.tim.67c": "large movement makes a loud sound and the opposite a soft one. Harmony between sounds must be dealt with later. There is a fourth kind of sensation still left, which has many varieties and must be divided up: as a whole we call them colours. Colour is a flame that comes off each body, with particles sized to the stream of sight so as to cause sensation. As for the stream of sight, they have already given the causes that produced it.",
  "greek.tim.67d": "About colours, the following is the most likely and sensible explanation. Of the particles that fly off bodies and strike the stream of sight, some are smaller than its own particles, some larger and some equal. The equal ones cannot be sensed, and we call them transparent. The larger and smaller ones, which respectively squeeze and widen the stream of sight, are like the hot and cold particles that affect the flesh,",
  "greek.tim.67e": "the astringent particles that affect the tongue, and the heating ones we call bitter. White and black are really the same kind of effect as these, in a different sense, though they seem different for the reasons given. So, Timaeus says, the names are: what widens the stream of sight is white, and the opposite black. And a faster movement, of a different kind of fire, strikes the stream of sight and widens it right up to the eyes, piercing",
  "greek.tim.68a": "and dissolving the passages of the eyes themselves, so that a mixture of fire and water pours out, which we call tears. This moving fire meets fire coming the other way; one stream leaps out like lightning, the other comes in and is quenched in the moisture, and in the mixture all sorts of colours appear. We call this feeling dazzle, and what causes it bright or brilliant. Again, when the kind of fire",
  "greek.tim.68b": "between these reaches the liquid of the eyes and mixes with it, it is not brilliant, but because the fire's ray is blended through the moisture it gives a blood-like colour, which we call red. Bright mixed with red and white becomes yellow. It would be foolish to say in what proportions the colours mix, Timaeus says, even if one knew, since no necessary or likely reason could properly be given. Red mixed with black and white makes purple;",
  "greek.tim.68c": "when these are mixed and more thoroughly burned, with black added, the result is violet. Chestnut comes from mixing yellow and grey; grey from white and black; ochre from white and yellow. White combined with bright and soaked in deep black turns dark blue; dark blue mixed with white becomes light blue; chestnut with black becomes green. From these examples it is fairly clear",
  "greek.tim.68d": "which mixtures the other colours should be matched with, if the account is to stay likely. But anyone who tried to test this by experiment would show he does not understand the difference between human nature and divine: God is wise and powerful enough to blend many things into one and to break one into many again, but no human being is, or ever will be, able to do either.",
  "greek.tim.68e": "These things, then, were necessarily like this, and the maker of the most beautiful and good took them over from among things that had come into being when he was making the self-sufficient and most perfect god. He used their built-in properties as helping causes, but he himself designed the good in everything being made. So, Timaeus says, one should distinguish two kinds of cause, the necessary and the divine. One should look for the divine in everything to gain a happy life, as far as our nature allows,",
  "greek.tim.69a": "and look for the necessary for the sake of the divine, realising that without the necessary we cannot see the divine things we strive for on their own, or grasp them, or share in them at all. Now that the different kinds of cause lie sorted before them, like timber ready for the carpenter, from which the rest of the account must be woven, Timaeus proposes to go back briefly to the starting point and then move quickly to the point",
  "greek.tim.69b": "they have now reached, so as to give the story an ending and a crown that fits what came before. As they said at the start, all these things were in disorder until God gave them proportions, each in itself and in relation to the others, as far as they could possibly be in harmony and proportion. Back then nothing had any proportion except by chance, and nothing deserved the names we now give, like fire and water",
  "greek.tim.69c": "or any of the others. He first put them all in order, and then from them built this universe, a single living creature containing all living creatures, mortal and immortal. He himself made the divine things, but he ordered his own sons to make the mortal ones. They copied him: taking the immortal beginning of the soul, they built a mortal body around it, gave it the whole body as its vehicle, and also housed in it another kind of soul, the mortal kind,",
  "greek.tim.69d": "which has terrible but unavoidable feelings in it: first pleasure, the strongest bait for evil; then pains, which drive good away; then rashness and fear, two foolish advisers; anger, hard to calm; and hope, easily led astray. Mixing these with unreasoning sensation and with lust that dares anything, they put together the mortal kind of soul as necessity required. And because they were reluctant to pollute the divine part except when absolutely necessary,",
  "greek.tim.69e": "they put the mortal kind somewhere else in the body, in a separate room. They built the neck between the head and chest as a narrow strip and boundary, to keep the two apart. Inside the chest, or thorax as it is called, they fastened the mortal kind of soul. And since one part of it is better and one worse, Timaeus says, they divided the chest cavity",
  "greek.tim.70a": "like separate rooms for men and for women, putting the midriff (diaphragm) between them as a screen. The part of the soul that has courage and spirit, since it loves to win, they put nearer the head, between the midriff and the neck, so that it could listen to reason and, together with reason, forcibly hold down the desires whenever they flatly refuse to obey the orders from reason's citadel. And the heart,",
  "greek.tim.70b": "where the veins meet and the source of the blood that rushes through all the limbs, they made the guardroom. That way, when anger boils up because reason has sent word that something wrong is being done to them, whether from outside or even from the desires within, every sense organ in the body could quickly pick up the orders and threats through all the channels and obey and follow them completely, letting the best part",
  "greek.tim.70c": "lead them all. And to relieve the pounding of the heart when danger is expected and anger is roused, since they knew that all such swelling of the passions would come from fire, they invented the lungs and put them in. The lungs are soft and bloodless, and full of holes like a sponge, so that when they take in breath and drink they cool things down and bring relief and comfort",
  "greek.tim.70d": "in the burning heat. For this purpose they ran the windpipe's channels to the lungs and placed the lungs around the heart as a kind of cushion, so that when anger was at its height, the heart could leap against something soft and be cooled, suffer less, and so serve reason better in times of passion. The part of the soul that craves food and drink and whatever else the body needs by nature, they put between the midriff and the boundary",
  "greek.tim.70e": "at the navel, making the whole region a sort of feeding trough for the body. There they tied up this part of the soul like a wild animal that still has to be kept attached to the rest and fed, if there are to be mortal beings at all. They did this, Timaeus says, so that this part, feeding at its trough and housed as far as possible from the part that thinks, making as little noise and disturbance as possible, would let the highest part think in peace",
  "greek.tim.71a": "about what is good for each and for all. That is why they put it there. They knew it would not understand reason, and even if it noticed reasons, it would have no natural urge to pay attention to them, but would mostly be spellbound day and night by images and illusions. To deal with this, God designed and made the liver and put it where that part lives.",
  "greek.tim.71b": "He made it dense, smooth, bright and sweet, but with some bitterness in it, so that the power of thoughts coming from the mind, moving in the liver as in a mirror that takes impressions and shows images, would frighten this part of the soul. When the mind comes down on it with stern threats, it uses the liver's related bitterness and quickly spreads it over the whole liver, giving it a bilious colour, and by tightening it makes it",
  "greek.tim.71c": "all wrinkled and rough. It bends the liver's lobe out of shape and squeezes it, and blocks and closes its passages and gates, causing pain and sickness. On the other hand, when a gentle breath from the mind paints the opposite kind of images on the liver, calms its bitterness by not stirring or touching what is opposite to it, and uses the liver's own sweetness on it",
  "greek.tim.71d": "to straighten all its parts and make them smooth and free, it makes the part of the soul living around the liver cheerful and calm, so that at night it behaves sensibly, busying itself in sleep with prophecy, since it has no share in reason and understanding. For those who made us remembered their Father's order to make the mortal kind",
  "greek.tim.71e": "as good as they possibly could, so they improved this low part of us by putting the organ of prophecy there, so that it might grasp some truth. A clear sign that God gave prophecy to human foolishness is this: nobody prophesies truly and with inspiration while in their right mind, only when their understanding is tied up in sleep, or disturbed by illness or by some divine inspiration. But it is for someone in their right mind, Timaeus says, to remember and think over what was said in dreams or waking visions by the prophetic, inspired nature, and all the visions seen, and by reasoning to work out",
  "greek.tim.72a": "what they mean and for whom they signal good or bad, in the future, past or present. It is not for someone who has been, and still is, in a frenzy to judge what he himself saw or said. As the old saying goes, only someone of sound mind can do his own business and know himself. That is why it is the custom to appoint prophets to judge",
  "greek.tim.72b": "these inspired predictions. Some people, who do not know any better, call them seers, but they are not seers; they are interpreters of the mysterious voices and visions, and would best be called 'prophets of things foretold'. So that is why the liver is as described and where it is: for the sake of prophecy. While the creature is alive, the liver gives fairly clear signs, but once it is dead, it goes blind and its signs are too murky to mean anything",
  "greek.tim.72c": "clear. The organ next to it, on the left, is there for the sake of the liver, to keep it always bright and clean, like a cloth kept ready beside a mirror. So whenever impurities from bodily illness gather around the liver, the loose-textured spleen cleans them up and absorbs them, because it is made of porous, bloodless stuff. When it fills with this waste, the spleen grows large and swollen;",
  "greek.tim.72d": "and when the body is cleansed, it goes down and shrinks back to its original size. As for the soul, which part is mortal and which immortal, where, with what companions and why they were housed separately: only if God agreed could they dare to say their account is true. But they must dare to say now that it is likely, and say so even more firmly as they go on. So let it be said.",
  "greek.tim.72e": "The next subject must be looked at in the same way: how the rest of the body came to be. Its making is best explained by reasoning like this. Those who were making our kind knew how little self-control we would have with food and drink, and that out of greed we would eat and drink far more than was moderate or needed. So, to stop disease quickly destroying them,",
  "greek.tim.73a": "and the mortal race coming to an end before it was even finished, the gods foresaw this and made the belly to hold the extra food and drink. Inside it they coiled the intestines, so that food would not pass through quickly and make the body need more food quickly, creating an endless appetite. Otherwise greed would make the whole race uninterested in philosophy and culture and disobedient to the most divine part of us. As for bones, flesh and all such substances,",
  "greek.tim.73b": "this is how it was. They all began with the making of the marrow. In the marrow were fastened the bonds of life that tie the soul to the body, and there the roots of the mortal kind were planted. But the marrow itself was made from other things. God took all the basic triangles that were straight and smooth and best able to make fire, water, air and earth exactly, and separated each from its own kind,",
  "greek.tim.73c": "and mixing them together in the right proportions, he made the marrow from them, as a seed-stuff for every mortal kind. Next he made the different kinds of soul in it and tied them down. Straight away he divided the marrow into shapes matching, in number and nature, the shapes the different kinds of soul would have. The part of the marrow that was to receive the divine seed, like a field, he shaped",
  "greek.tim.73d": "into a perfect ball and called it the brain, meaning that, when each creature was finished, the container around it would be called the head. The part that was to hold the other, mortal part of the soul he divided into shapes both round and long, and called all of it marrow. From these, as from anchors, he cast out ropes to tie on the whole soul, and around it he finally built our whole body, after first building a shelter around it made",
  "greek.tim.73e": "entirely of bone. He made bone like this. He sifted earth until it was pure and smooth, kneaded it and wetted it with marrow. Then he put it in fire, then dipped it in water, then back into fire and again into water, and by moving it back and forth many times he made it so that neither could dissolve it. Using this, Timaeus says, he turned a ball of bone around the brain, leaving a narrow opening; and around the marrow",
  "greek.tim.74a": "of the neck and back he shaped bony vertebrae and set them like hinges in an upright row all down the trunk, starting from the head. So, to protect the whole seed, he fenced it in with a stony ring, and put joints in it, using the power of the Other as a go-between, so that it could move and bend.",
  "greek.tim.74b": "He thought bone was too hard and stiff, and that if it were heated and cooled again it would decay and soon destroy the seed inside. So he invented sinew and flesh. With sinew, which tightens and loosens around the hinges, he meant to tie all the limbs together so the body could bend and stretch. Flesh was to be a shield against heat and a shelter against cold, and when we fall, to give way softly and gently, like padded clothing;",
  "greek.tim.74c": "and since it holds warm moisture, in summer it would sweat and keep the whole outside of the body pleasantly cool, while in winter its fire would protect the body well enough from the frost attacking it from outside. With this in mind, our Modeller mixed water, fire and earth together, made a ferment of acid and salt",
  "greek.tim.74d": "and mixed it in, and so made flesh, juicy and soft. He made sinew from a mix of bone and unfermented flesh, a single substance in between the two, and coloured it with yellow. That is why sinews are firmer and stiffer than flesh but softer and more flexible than bone. With these God wrapped the bones and marrow, first tying them together with sinews and then covering them all with flesh.",
  "greek.tim.74e": "The bones with the most soul he covered with the least flesh, and the bones with the least soul with the most and densest flesh. At the joints he grew only a little flesh, unless reason showed a need for it, so that it would not get in the way of bending and make the body clumsy and stiff, or, by being thick and dense, make it numb and so make the thinking parts more forgetful and dull. That is why, Timaeus says,",
  "greek.tim.75a": "the thighs, shins, hips, upper and lower arms, and all our other parts without joints, and all the bones with little intelligence inside because the marrow holds little soul, are well covered with flesh. The thinking parts have less, except where he made flesh that can itself carry sensations, as with the tongue; but most of these he made as described. For the stuff that is produced by necessity and grows up with us",
  "greek.tim.75b": "will not allow sharp perception to go together with dense bone and lots of flesh. If they could go together, the head would have had them most of all, and humans, with fleshy, sinewy, strong heads, would have lived twice, or many times, as long as now, and been healthier and freer from pain. But as it is, when our makers were considering",
  "greek.tim.75c": "whether to make a race that lived longer but was worse, or one that lived shorter but was better, they agreed that everyone should by all means choose the shorter, better life over the longer, worse one. So they covered the head with thin bone but not with flesh and sinews, since it does not bend either. For all these reasons, every person's head, joined to the body, was more perceptive and intelligent but less strong. On these grounds and in this way, God put the sinews at the bottom of the head,",
  "greek.tim.75d": "around the neck, and glued them evenly there. With them he fastened the ends of the jaws below the face, and spread the rest of the sinews among all the limbs, tying joint to joint. Those who made our mouth gave it teeth, tongue and lips, as it is now,",
  "greek.tim.75e": "for purposes both necessary and best. They made it a way in for what is necessary and a way out for what is best. Everything that goes in to feed the body is necessary, while the stream of speech flowing out and serving intelligence is the finest and best of all streams. Also, Timaeus says, the head could not be left as bare bone, because of the extreme heat and cold of the seasons; nor could it be wrapped up and made stupid and numb by a heavy mass of flesh.",
  "greek.tim.76a": "So from the fleshy substance that was not fully dried out, a larger outer film was separated off, which we now call skin. It joined up with itself because of the moisture around the brain and spread out to cover the head. The moisture rising under the seams of the skull kept it damp, and it closed over the crown, gathered as if in a knot. The seams took all sorts of shapes because of the force of the soul's circles and its food: more seams where these clash more, fewer where they clash less.",
  "greek.tim.76b": "God kept piercing this skin all over with fire. When the skin was pierced and moisture escaped, all the pure liquid and heat went away, but the part mixed with the stuff the skin was made of was carried up by the motion and stretched out far beyond the skin, as fine as the hole. But because it was slow, the outside air pushed it back, so it curled round inside and took root under the skin.",
  "greek.tim.76c": "That is how hair grew in the skin: a stringy kind of thing related to skin but harder and denser, because the cold squeezed it; each hair was chilled and tightened as it separated from the skin. Using these causes, our Maker made the head covered with hair, intending it to serve, instead of flesh, as a light roof over the brain for safety,",
  "greek.tim.76d": "giving enough shade and shelter in summer and winter while not getting in the way of perception. Where sinew, skin and bone were woven together at the fingers, a material formed from all three, which dried into a single hard skin. These were the helping causes, but it was made by the greatest cause, divine purpose, for the sake of what would happen later. Those making us knew that from men would one day come women",
  "greek.tim.76e": "and all the other animals, and that many of these creatures would need nails for many purposes. So they stamped the beginnings of fingernails on men right from their birth. For these reasons and plans they made skin grow into hair and nails at the ends of the limbs. When all the limbs and parts of the mortal creature had been joined together,",
  "greek.tim.77a": "it had to live in fire and air, and so it wasted away when these broke it down or drained it. So the gods came up with help. They mixed a substance related to human nature with other shapes and senses to make another kind of living thing: the cultivated trees, plants and seeds, now tamed by farming and living among us. Before, only the wild kinds existed,",
  "greek.tim.77b": "which are older than the cultivated ones. Anything that shares in life can rightly and truly be called a living creature. The one being described has the third kind of soul, which sits between the midriff and the navel. It has no opinion, reasoning or mind, but has sensation, pleasant and painful, and desires. Because it is entirely passive and does not turn within itself around itself, pushing away motion from outside",
  "greek.tim.77c": "and using its own motion, it was not made with any natural ability to judge or reflect on what happens to it. So it is alive and is a living creature, but it stays still and rooted because it cannot move itself. When our superiors had made all these kinds as food for us lesser beings, they dug channels through our bodies, like channels cut in a garden, so the body could be watered by an inflowing stream. First, under the join",
  "greek.tim.77d": "of skin and flesh, they cut two hidden veins down the back, since the body is double, with a right and a left side. They ran them down beside the spine, with the seed-bearing marrow between them, so that it would thrive as much as possible, and so that the moisture from there, flowing downhill, would reach the other parts easily and water them evenly. After this",
  "greek.tim.77e": "they split the veins around the head and wove them together in opposite directions, bending those from the right of the head to the left and those from the left to the right. This was so that, together with the skin, they would tie the head to the body, since the crown of the head has no ring of sinews, and also so that sensations from each side would be felt by the whole body.",
  "greek.tim.78a": "Then they set up the watering system on a plan like this, which will be easier to see once they agree on some points. Everything made of smaller particles can hold in larger ones, but what is made of larger particles cannot hold in smaller ones. Fire, which has the smallest particles of all, passes through water, earth, air and everything made of them, and nothing can hold it in. The same rule must apply to our belly. When food and drink flow into it,",
  "greek.tim.78b": "it holds them in, but it cannot hold in air and fire, whose particles are smaller than its own structure. So God used these to water the veins from the belly. He wove from air and fire a net like a fish-trap, with two funnels at its entrance, one of which he split in two again. From the funnels he stretched what looked like ropes all round to the edges of the net. The inside of the net",
  "greek.tim.78c": "he made entirely of fire, and the funnels and the outer casing of air. He put this around the living creature like this. He let the funnel part down into the mouth; since it was double, he sent one funnel down the windpipe into the lungs, and the other into the belly beside the windpipe. He split the first in two and gave both parts a shared outlet through the nose, so that when the first route, through the mouth, was not working,",
  "greek.tim.78d": "its streams could be refilled through the nose. The rest of the casing net he made grow around the whole hollow of our body. He made all of it sometimes flow gently into the funnels, since they were made of air, and sometimes the funnels flow back into it. And since the body was porous, he made the net pass in and out through it, with the rays of fire inside following the air in both directions. So, as long as the mortal creature holds together, this goes on without stopping.",
  "greek.tim.78e": "The one who gave things their names called this process breathing in and breathing out. The whole mechanism and its effects were made to feed and keep our body alive by moistening and cooling it. As the breath goes in and out, Timaeus says, the fire inside goes with it, and whenever, in its constant swinging back and forth, this fire enters through the belly",
  "greek.tim.79a": "and takes hold of food and drink, it breaks them down into small particles, spreads them through the outlets it passes along and draws them into the veins, like water drawn into channels from a spring. So it makes the streams in the veins flow through the body as if through a pipe. Timaeus proposes to look again at breathing and the reasons it works as it does.",
  "greek.tim.79b": "It works like this. Since there is no empty space for any moving body to go into, when our breath goes out the result is clear to everyone: it does not go into empty space but pushes the body next to it out of its place. That body pushes the next, and by necessity each is driven round towards the place the breath left, enters it and fills it, following the breath. All of this happens at once, like a turning wheel, because there is no empty space.",
  "greek.tim.79c": "So when the chest and lungs let out breath, they are filled again by the air around the body, which seeps in through the porous flesh and goes round. And when that air is pushed back and out through the body, it drives the breathed-in air round and in through the mouth and nostrils. The starting cause",
  "greek.tim.79d": "of all this, Timaeus says, is this. Every living creature is extremely hot inside, around the blood and veins, as if it had a spring of fire within. This is the part they compared to the casing of the fish-trap, saying that everything stretched through its middle was woven of fire, and the outer parts of air. Heat naturally goes out to its own region and its own kind. Since there are two ways out, one through the body",
  "greek.tim.79e": "and the other through the mouth and nose, whenever the fire rushes one way it pushes the air round to the other. The air pushed round gets heated by flowing into the fire, while the air going out gets cooled. As the heat shifts and the particles at the other outlet get hotter, the hotter air heads that way in turn, moving towards its own kind, and pushes round the air at the first outlet. So the air, always undergoing and passing on the same changes, produces breathing in and out by this double process, like a wheel rocking back and forth. In the same way, Timaeus says, they should trace the causes of what happens with medical cupping-glasses,",
  "greek.tim.80a": "with swallowing, and with thrown objects, whether thrown upward or flying along the ground; and of all sounds that seem high or low because they are fast or slow, which sometimes clash because of the uneven motion they cause in us and sometimes agree because the motion is even. The slower sounds catch up with the motions of the earlier, faster sounds when these begin to stop",
  "greek.tim.80b": "and have already slowed to the speed at which the slower sounds hit and move them. When the slower catch up with the faster, they do not upset them by imposing a different motion, but add to them the start of a slower motion matching the faster one that is dying away. So from high and low they blend a single sensation, which gives pleasure to the unintelligent and, to the intelligent, the deeper delight of seeing divine harmony imitated in mortal motions. Also, as for all flowing water, falling",
  "greek.tim.80c": "thunderbolts, and the marvels of the pull of electron and the Heraclean stone: none of these has any real power of attraction. Rather, there is no empty space, these bodies push each other round, and as they separate or come together they all swap places and each heads to its own region. It is through these complicated, interlocking processes that such wonders happen, as anyone who looks into them properly will see.",
  "greek.tim.80d": "And breathing, where this account began, came about as they said before, in this way and by these means. The fire breaks down the food and rises through the body following the breath; as it rises with the breath, it fills the veins from the belly by drawing the broken-down particles into them. That is why, in all living creatures, streams of nourishment run like this through the whole body. And since these nourishing particles are freshly broken down",
  "greek.tim.80e": "and come from related substances, some from fruit and some from grain, which God planted for us specifically as food, they take on all sorts of colours as they mix. But red runs through them most of all, because the fire naturally makes it as it breaks down the liquid food and stamps itself on it. That is why, Timaeus says, the stream flowing through the body looks as described. We call this stream blood, and it is the food of the flesh",
  "greek.tim.81a": "and of the whole body. Each part draws fluid from it and fills the gap left by what has gone out. This filling and emptying happens the same way as all motion in the universe, by the rule that everything moves towards its own kind. The bodies around us outside are always breaking us down and sending off to each kind of substance what belongs to it. And the blood particles, chopped up inside us and surrounded by each creature's frame as if by a heaven, have to copy the motion of the whole;",
  "greek.tim.81b": "so, when each of the particles broken up inside moves towards its own kind, it fills up the emptied place again. When more goes out than comes in, a creature wastes away; when less, it grows. When a creature's whole frame is new, its basic triangles are still fresh, as if just off the shipbuilder's stocks, and hold tightly together, and the whole mass is soft,",
  "greek.tim.81c": "since it has just been made from marrow and fed on milk. The triangles in its food and drink, coming in from outside, are older and weaker than its own, so it cuts them up and overcomes them with its own new triangles, and so makes the creature grow by feeding it on many similar things. But when the roots of the triangles grow slack from fighting many battles over a long time,",
  "greek.tim.81d": "they can no longer cut up the triangles coming in with the food and make them like themselves, but are easily cut up by them. In this state every animal is overcome and wastes away, and this is called old age. Finally, when the bonds of the triangles in the marrow, which were well fitted together, can no longer hold under the strain and come apart, they let go the bonds of the soul too, and the soul, set free naturally, flies off gladly;",
  "greek.tim.81e": "for everything against nature is painful, and what happens naturally is pleasant. In the same way, death from disease or wounds is painful and violent, but death that comes with old age as a natural end is the least distressing of deaths, with more pleasure than pain. The origin of disease, Timaeus says, is obvious to everyone.",
  "greek.tim.82a": "The body is made of four elements: earth, fire, water and air. When, against nature, there is too much or too little of them, or they move from their own place to a foreign one; or, since fire and the others each come in more than one variety, whenever the body takes in an unsuitable variety, these and similar things cause internal disorder and disease. When any element changes against nature, all its particles that used to be cooled",
  "greek.tim.82b": "become heated, dry ones become moist, light ones heavy, and they change in every possible way. For only when the same substance is added to or taken from the same substance in the same order, in the same way and in the right proportion can it stay safe and healthy and true to itself. Anything that breaks any of these rules in going out or coming in will cause all kinds of changes and countless diseases and decay.",
  "greek.tim.82c": "Again, in the structures made second in order, there is a second class of diseases for anyone who wants to notice them. Since marrow, bone, flesh and sinew are made from the elements, and blood from the same things in a different way, most other illnesses happen like those already described. But the worst are dangerous for this reason: whenever these second substances are produced in the reverse order, they go bad. In the natural order, flesh and sinews come from blood:",
  "greek.tim.82d": "sinew from the fibrin, because they are related, and flesh from what clots when it separates from the fibrin. Further, the sticky, oily substance that comes from sinew and flesh not only glues the flesh to the bones but also feeds and builds up the bone around the marrow; while the part made of the purest, smoothest, oiliest triangles seeps through",
  "greek.tim.82e": "the dense bones, and as it oozes and drips from them it moistens the marrow. When each of these is produced in this order, the usual result is health; in the reverse order, disease. Whenever flesh breaks down and sends its decayed matter back into the veins, it mixes with air, and the blood in the veins, plentiful and of every kind, takes on all sorts of colours and bitter tastes, and sharp and salty qualities, and contains bile, serum and every sort of phlegm. When everything is reversed and spoiled, Timaeus says, it first destroys the blood itself, and then stops giving",
  "greek.tim.83a": "any nourishment to the body. These substances move through the veins in every direction and no longer keep the order of their natural circuits. They are at odds with themselves, getting no good from each other, and at war with the body's settled, orderly make-up, which they spoil and break down. So the oldest decayed flesh becomes tough and blackened from long burning, and because it is eaten away all round it is bitter, and so dangerous",
  "greek.tim.83b": "when it attacks any part of the body not yet spoiled. Sometimes the black matter becomes sharp instead of bitter, when the bitter stuff is watered down; sometimes the bitter stuff turns redder from being soaked in blood, and if black matter mixes with it, it turns greenish. And whenever new flesh too is broken down by the fire of inflammation, a yellow matter mixes with the bitter stuff.",
  "greek.tim.83c": "All these fluids have been given the general name bile, either by some doctors or by someone able to look at many different cases and see in them one type worth naming them all after. All the other kinds counted as bile got their particular names from their colours. Serum comes in two kinds: one is the mild watery part of blood; the other, from black and acid bile, is harmful when heat makes it salty, and is called acid phlegm. Another kind contains air and comes from the breaking down of new, tender flesh. When this is puffed up",
  "greek.tim.83d": "and enclosed in liquid, so that bubbles form that are each too small to see but together make a visible mass, white-looking because of the froth, all this broken-down tender flesh mixed with air is called white phlegm. The watery part of newly formed phlegm is sweat and tears,",
  "greek.tim.83e": "and all the other fluids that pour out in the body's daily cleansing. All of these become causes of disease whenever the blood is not refilled naturally from food and drink but gets its bulk from the wrong substances, against nature's laws. When flesh somewhere is being broken down by disease but its foundations stay firm, Timaeus says, the attack is only half as strong, since it can still recover easily;",
  "greek.tim.84a": "but when the substance that binds flesh to bone becomes diseased, and no longer separates readily from them and the sinews to feed the bone and bind flesh to bone, but, starved by a bad way of life, becomes rough and salty instead of oily, smooth and sticky, then all of it, as this happens, rots away under the flesh and sinews",
  "greek.tim.84b": "and comes away from the bones. The flesh falls away with it from its roots, leaving the sinews bare and full of salty matter, and by falling back into the bloodstream it makes the illnesses already described worse. But though these bodily illnesses are severe, worse still are those that come before them: when the bone, because the flesh is too dense, does not get enough air, overheats, goes mouldy and rots, and does not take in its food, but instead falls back itself,",
  "greek.tim.84c": "crumbling, into its food, which then passes into the flesh, and the flesh falling into the blood makes all such illnesses more violent than those described before. The most extreme case of all is when the marrow becomes diseased from too little or too much; this causes the worst and deadliest diseases, because the whole substance of the body is forced to flow in reverse. A third class of diseases, Timaeus says, comes about in three ways:",
  "greek.tim.84d": "partly from air, partly from phlegm and partly from bile. When the lungs, which hand out air to the body, are blocked with discharges and cannot keep their outlets clear, the air cannot get through one way and comes in another way in larger amounts than it should. It makes the parts cut off from breathing rot, and forces and twists the veins, and as it breaks down the body it gets shut up in the middle of it, around the midriff. As a result",
  "greek.tim.84e": "countless painful diseases are caused, with heavy sweating. Often, when the flesh breaks down, air trapped inside the body that cannot get out causes the same agonies as air coming in from outside. These are worst when the air surrounds the sinews and nearby veins and, by swelling, pulls back the tendons and the sinews attached to them; it is from this intense straining that these illnesses got their names, tetanus and opisthotonus. The cure for these is harsh too, Timaeus says: what helps most is a bout of fever.",
  "greek.tim.85a": "White phlegm is also dangerous when it is trapped inside by the air in its bubbles. When it has vents to the outside it is milder, though it marks the body with spots, breeding white scabs, rashes and similar illnesses. When this phlegm mixes with black bile and spreads over the circles of the head, which are the most divine, and disturbs them,",
  "greek.tim.85b": "it is gentler during sleep, but harder to shake off when it attacks people who are awake. Because it is a disease of the sacred part, it is very rightly called the sacred disease. Sharp, salty phlegm is the source of all illnesses like catarrhs (runny discharges), which have all kinds of names because they flow into all kinds of places. All the diseases called inflammations, because the body burns and flares up, are caused by bile. When bile finds a way out,",
  "greek.tim.85c": "it boils over and throws up all sorts of eruptions on the skin; but when it is shut inside, it causes many burning diseases. The worst is when the bile, mixed with pure blood, pushes the fibrin out of its proper place. The fibrin is spread through the blood so that the blood has the right balance of thinness and thickness, and does not run out of the porous body because heat has made it too runny, nor become too thick",
  "greek.tim.85d": "to move and struggle to flow through the veins. The fibrin keeps the right balance because of how it is formed. Even if you gather the fibrin out of blood that is dead and cooling, all the rest of the blood stays liquid; but if the fibrin is left in, it works with the surrounding cold and quickly clots the blood. Since fibrin works like this, bile, which is naturally made from old blood and turned back into blood from flesh, seeps into the blood bit by bit at first, while hot and moist,",
  "greek.tim.85e": "and is clotted by the fibrin; as it clots and is forcibly chilled, it causes cold and shivering inside. But when bile floods in more heavily, Timaeus says, its heat overpowers the fibrin and throws it into disorder by boiling. If it can keep overpowering the fibrin, it reaches the marrow and burns through the soul's mooring ropes, as if loosing a ship, and sets it free. But when there is less bile and the body resists being broken down, the bile itself is beaten and is either pushed out all over the surface of the body or forced through the veins into the lower or upper belly, driven out of the body like refugees from a city in revolt;",
  "greek.tim.86a": "and it causes diarrhoea, dysentery and similar illnesses. When the body falls ill mainly from too much fire, it has constant inflammations and fevers; from air, daily fevers; from water, fevers every third day, because water is slower than air or fire; and from earth, the fourth and slowest element, which is cleared out in four-day cycles, it gets fevers every fourth day and is hard to cure.",
  "greek.tim.86b": "That is how diseases of the body happen. Diseases of the soul caused by the state of the body arise like this. We must agree that folly is a disease of the soul, and there are two kinds of folly: madness and ignorance. Whatever someone suffers, if it involves either, it must be called disease. And excessive pleasures and pains must be counted the greatest diseases of the soul. When someone is overjoyed, or on the other hand suffering terribly",
  "greek.tim.86c": "from pain, rushing too eagerly to grab one and escape the other, he cannot see or hear anything properly; he is beside himself and quite unable to reason. And when a man's seed builds up in large amounts in his marrow, like a tree overloaded with fruit, he brings on himself, again and again, many pangs and many pleasures from his desires and what comes of them, and ends up mad",
  "greek.tim.86d": "for most of his life because of these greatest of pleasures and pains, keeping his soul sick and senseless because of what his body does. Yet people think such a man is wicked by choice, not ill. In truth, this lack of sexual self-control, which comes mostly from having too much of one substance, made runny by porous bones, is a disease of the soul. In fact, nearly all the things that people blame as lack of control over pleasure, as if the wicked chose to be so, are wrongly blamed; for no one is wicked by choice,",
  "greek.tim.86e": "but a wicked man becomes wicked because of some bad bodily condition and poor upbringing, things everyone hates and nobody chooses. Likewise, the soul gets much harm from the body through pains. Timaeus explains: whenever the fluids from acid and salty phlegm, and all bitter, bilious fluids, wander through the body and find no way out",
  "greek.tim.87a": "but are shut inside, and mix their vapour with the soul's movement, they plant all kinds of diseases in the soul, of differing strength and extent. Depending on which of the soul's three regions they reach, they cause every kind of bad temper and low spirits, all kinds of rashness and cowardice, and forgetfulness and stupidity too. On top of that, when people are in such a bad state,",
  "greek.tim.87b": "and the government is bad too, and the talk in the cities, public and private, is bad, and nowhere are children taught lessons that would cure these evils, then all of us who are wicked become so through two causes we never chose. For these we should always blame the parents more than the children, and the teachers more than the pupils. Still, each person must try as hard as he can, through upbringing, pursuits and study, to escape evil and pursue good. But that, Timaeus says, is a separate subject.",
  "greek.tim.87c": "Next it is right to discuss the other side: how to care for body and mind and what keeps them healthy, since the good deserves describing more than the bad. Everything good is beautiful, and the beautiful is not without proportion; so a living creature that is to be beautiful must be well proportioned. We notice and reason about small proportions, but we have no rational grasp of the most important and greatest ones. When it comes to health and disease,",
  "greek.tim.87d": "goodness and badness, no proportion or lack of it matters more than that between the soul and the body themselves. Yet we completely fail to see or consider that when a weak, poor body carries a strong, great soul, or the other way round, the creature as a whole is not beautiful, because it lacks the most important proportion. A creature in the opposite state is, for anyone with eyes to see, the most beautiful",
  "greek.tim.87e": "and admirable of sights. For example, a body with legs too long, or out of proportion in some other way, is not only ugly but, when it has to work with others, causes a lot of tiredness, many sprains and falls through its clumsy movements, and so brings countless troubles on itself. We must think the same, Timaeus says, about the combination of soul and body we call a living creature. Whenever the soul in it is stronger than the body",
  "greek.tim.88a": "and very excited, it shakes the whole body from inside and fills it with illness. When the soul throws itself into some study or research, it wears the body out. And when it takes part in teaching or arguments, in public or private, full of dispute and rivalry, it heats the body up, shakes it apart and brings on catarrhs, fooling most so-called doctors into blaming the wrong cause. On the other hand, when a big, overbearing body is joined to a small",
  "greek.tim.88b": "and weak mind, then, since people naturally have two desires, for food for the body's sake and for wisdom for the sake of our most divine part, the movements of the stronger part win and grow stronger, while they make the soul dull, slow and forgetful, and so produce in it the worst disease of all, ignorance. The one way to escape both evils is not to exercise the soul without the body or the body without the soul, so that they stay evenly matched and healthy. So the mathematician,",
  "greek.tim.88c": "or anyone who works hard with his mind at any subject, must also exercise his body through gymnastics; while someone who works hard at building his body must in turn exercise his soul through music and philosophy in general, if either is to deserve to be called truly beautiful and good. The different parts must be treated the same way, copying the pattern of the universe. For the body",
  "greek.tim.88d": "is heated or chilled inside by the particles that enter it, and dried or moistened by those outside, and suffers the effects of both. When someone hands his body over to these motions while keeping it still, it is overwhelmed and destroyed. But if he copies what they called the nurse and foster-mother of the universe, never letting the body rest if he can help it, keeping it moving, and with constant inner shaking protecting it in nature's way against the motions inside and outside, then through moderate shaking",
  "greek.tim.88e": "he arranges the feelings and particles wandering about the body in their proper order according to their kinship, as described in the earlier account of the universe. Then he will not let enemy be placed beside enemy to breed war and disease in the body, but will put friend beside friend, producing good health.",
  "greek.tim.89a": "As for movement, Timaeus says, the best movement for a body is the one it makes itself, within itself, because that is closest to the movement of intelligence and of the universe. Movement caused by something else is less good, and the worst is movement given bit by bit by outside agents to a body lying still. So the best movement for cleansing and renewing the body is physical exercise; second best is being rocked in vehicles like boats or any transport that does not tire you. The third kind may help someone who has no choice,",
  "greek.tim.89b": "but otherwise no sensible person should accept it: the doctor's cleansing by drugs. Diseases that are not very dangerous should not be stirred up with drugs. Every disease is put together rather like a living creature. Living creatures as a whole have set life spans for their species, and each individual also has a natural, fixed length of life,",
  "greek.tim.89c": "apart from unavoidable accidents. From the very start, each creature's triangles are built to last up to a certain time, beyond which no one could live. The same rule applies to the make-up of diseases. Whenever someone forces a disease with drugs, against its set time, many serious diseases usually follow instead of a few mild ones. So one should manage all such diseases, as far as",
  "greek.tim.89d": "one has the time for it, by diet rather than stirring up a troublesome evil with drugs. That is enough, Timaeus says, about the whole living creature and its bodily part, and how a person should guide and be guided by himself to live most reasonably. But first, and with special care, they must prepare the part that is to do the guiding, to make it as beautiful and good as possible for the job. To explain this subject alone in exact detail would be",
  "greek.tim.89e": "a big enough task in itself. But treating it as a side issue and following their earlier account, they can reasonably put their conclusions like this. Timaeus reminds them that they have often said that three kinds of soul live in three regions of us, each with its own movements. So now they must repeat, as briefly as possible, that whichever kind stays idle and keeps its movements at rest will become weakest, while the kind that exercises becomes strongest;",
  "greek.tim.90a": "so care must be taken that their movements are kept in proportion to each other. As for the most lordly part of our soul, Timaeus says God has given it to each of us as a guiding spirit (daemon): the part housed at the top of the body, which lifts us up from earth towards our kin in heaven, since we are a heavenly plant, not an earthly one. This is very true, he says, because by hanging our head, our root, from the place the soul first came from, the divine power",
  "greek.tim.90b": "keeps our whole body upright. So anyone who gives himself over to lusts or quarrels and devotes himself too much to them is bound to be full of entirely mortal thoughts, and to become as mortal as anyone can, not falling short in the least, since he has made his mortal part grow great. But anyone who has seriously devoted himself to learning and true thinking, and has exercised these above everything else,",
  "greek.tim.90c": "is bound to think immortal and divine thoughts, if he grasps the truth. As far as human nature can share in immortality, he will not fall short at all. And since he is always caring for his divine part and keeping well the guiding spirit that lives with him, he will be supremely happy. There is one way to care for any part: give it its own proper food and movement. For the divine part in us, the movements that suit it",
  "greek.tim.90d": "are the thoughts and circlings of the universe. Each of us should follow these, correcting the circles in our head that were knocked out of line at birth, by learning the harmonies and circlings of the universe. In this way we make the thinking part like what it thinks about, as it was by nature at first, and once we have become like it, we reach the goal the gods have set for people as the best life, now and in the time to come.",
  "greek.tim.90e": "Timaeus says the task set at the start, describing the universe up to the making of humans, seems nearly finished. How the other living creatures came about needs only a short account, as there is no need to go on at length; keeping it short will keep things in proportion. So he puts it like this. According to the likely account, all those born as men who turned out cowardly and spent their lives doing wrong were changed",
  "greek.tim.91a": "into women at their second birth. That is why the gods then created sexual desire, making one kind of living thing in men and another in women, like this. From the outlet for drink, where the fluid that has passed through the lungs, under the kidneys and into the bladder, squeezed by air, is let out, they bored a hole into the dense marrow that runs from the head down the neck and along the spine,",
  "greek.tim.91b": "the marrow they earlier called seed. Because this marrow is alive and has been given an outlet, it has filled the place of the outlet with a love of begetting, planting there a strong desire to release. That is why in men the genitals are disobedient and wilful, like an animal that will not listen to reason, and try to take control of everything because of their frantic lusts.",
  "greek.tim.91c": "In women, for the same reasons, the womb, as it is called, is a living thing inside them that longs to bear children. When it stays barren long past the right time, it gets upset and takes it badly; it wanders all over the body, blocks the breathing passages and stops breathing, throwing the body into the worst distress and causing all sorts of illnesses, until the desire and love of the two sexes bring them together. Then, like picking fruit from trees,",
  "greek.tim.91d": "they sow in the womb, as in ploughed ground, tiny creatures too small to see and still unformed. These are then shaped and fed until they grow large inside the body, and afterwards brought out into the light, and so the birth of the living creature is complete. That is how women and the whole female sex came into being. Birds came from harmless but light-minded men who, studying the things above, foolishly thought that the surest proofs about them come from eyesight; they were transformed, growing feathers instead of hair.",
  "greek.tim.91e": "Wild land animals came from men who paid no attention to philosophy and never studied the heavens, because they stopped using the circles in their heads and followed the parts of the soul in the chest. Because of these habits, Timaeus says, they dragged their front limbs and heads down to the earth, which they are akin to, and planted them there, and got long heads of every shape, depending on how their circles had been bent out of shape through lack of use.",
  "greek.tim.92a": "That is also why their kind was made four-footed or many-footed: God put more supports under the more foolish ones, so they would be dragged even closer to the earth. And since the most foolish of these, stretched out full length on the ground, no longer needed feet at all, the gods made them footless, wriggling along the earth.",
  "greek.tim.92b": "The fourth kind, which lives in water, came from the most thoroughly thoughtless and stupid men. Those who remade them thought them no longer worthy even of breathing pure air, because their souls were unclean with every kind of wickedness. So instead of fine, clean air, they pushed them into water to breathe its murky depths. From them came fish, shellfish and all water creatures, who got the lowest home of all as payment for the depth of their stupidity. So, then and now, living creatures keep changing",
  "greek.tim.92c": "into one another in all these ways, as they lose or gain reason and folly. And now at last, Timaeus says, their account of the universe has reached its end. This cosmos has received living creatures, mortal and immortal, and been filled with them. It is itself a visible living creature that holds all the visible ones, a god that can be perceived, made in the image of the intelligible one, the greatest, best, most beautiful and most perfect: this one Heaven, the only one of its kind.",
};
